  "contracts": {
    "def-qft-photon-transverse-space": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let\n$(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space and let\n$L^2(\\mu)$ be the quotient of $\\mathcal L^2(\\mu)$ by the almost-everywhere zero\nfunctions ([[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]]), with the quotient\nnorm $\\|\\cdot\\|_2$ ([[thm-the-l-p-norm-descends-to-the-quotient-and-makes-l-p-a-normed-space]]).\n\n1. On **real** $L^2(\\mu)$ the formula\n   $$\\langle f,g\\rangle:=\\int fg\\,d\\mu$$is a well-defined inner product, linear in both variables, positive definite, with $\\langle f,f\\rangle=\\|f\\|_2^2$; with it $L^2(\\mu)$ is a real Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]). 2. On **complex** $L^2(\\mu;\\mathbb C)$ the formula$$\\langle f,g\\rangle:=\\int f\\overline g\\,d\\mu$$\n   is a well-defined inner product, linear in the first variable and\n   conjugate-linear in the second, positive definite, with\n   $\\langle f,f\\rangle=\\|f\\|_2^2$; with it $L^2(\\mu;\\mathbb C)$ is a complex\n   Hilbert space ([[def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions]]).\n\nIn both cases the inner product induces exactly the established quotient\n$L^2$ norm.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For an integer $d\\ge1$, let $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$ be the complex Schwartz space with seminorms\n$p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)=\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb R^d}|x^\\alpha\\partial^\\beta f(x)|$\nfor all multi-indices $\\alpha,\\beta$; this is exactly\n[[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]]. Let\n$\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^d)=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ with the support convention of\n[[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]] and the locally convex LF topology of\n[[def-test-function-topology]]. The tempered-distribution space $\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^d)$\nis the space of continuous complex-linear functionals on this topological\n$\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$, paired bilinearly with test functions, as in\n[[def-tempered-distribution]].\n\nFor every complex Hilbert space $H$, use the second-variable-linear pairing\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_2:=(v,u)_1$, where $(\\cdot,\\cdot)_1$ is the first-variable-linear\nHilbert pairing of [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]. Thus\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_2$ is linear in $v$ and conjugate-linear in $u$.\nFor each tensor product, use the same norm completion as\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction]], but equip it with the\nsecond-variable-linear pairing obtained by conjugating that supplier's\nfirst-variable-linear pairing. This leaves the norm and completion unchanged;\non elementary tensors it gives the product of the second-variable pairings.\nLet $H^{\\widehat\\otimes n}$ be the iterated Hilbert tensor completion, with\n$H^{\\widehat\\otimes0}:=\\mathbb C$; the tensor inner product is the product of\nthe factor pairings. For $n\\ge1$, let\n$P_{s,n}=n!^{-1}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}U_\\pi$ and\n$P_{a,n}=n!^{-1}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\pi)U_\\pi$, using the\npermutation action and the orthogonal projections of\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections]]. Define\n$H_s^{(n)}=\\operatorname{ran}P_{s,n}$ and\n$H_a^{(n)}=\\operatorname{ran}P_{a,n}$, with\n$H_s^{(0)}=H_a^{(0)}=\\mathbb C$. These are the symmetric and antisymmetric\n$n$-particle sectors.\n\nThe bosonic and fermionic Fock Hilbert spaces are the square-summable direct\nsums\n$\\mathcal F_s(H)=\\{(\\Psi_n)_{n\\ge0}:\\Psi_n\\in H_s^{(n)},\\\n\\sum_n\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}$ and\n$\\mathcal F_a(H)=\\{(\\Psi_n)_{n\\ge0}:\\Psi_n\\in H_a^{(n)},\\\n\\sum_n\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}$, with the componentwise inner product.\nTheir vacuum is $\\Omega=(1,0,0,\\ldots)$. The finite-particle subspace\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ consists of sequences with only finitely many nonzero\ncomponents.\n\nFor $n\\ge0$, bosonic creation on sector $n$ is\n$a_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt{n+1}P_{s,n+1}(h\\otimes\\Psi_n)$; for $n\\ge1$,\nbosonic annihilation is\n$a_n(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt n(C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)})\\Psi_n$, where\n$C_h(v)=\\langle h,v\\rangle_2$ and its tensor extension is the bounded map of\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-bounded-tensor-maps]], and $a(h)\\Omega=0$. Define fermionic creation\nby $c_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt{n+1}P_{a,n+1}(h\\otimes\\Psi_n)$; for $n\\ge1$,\nfermionic annihilation is the corresponding first-factor contraction\n$c_n(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt n(C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)})\\Psi_n$, and\n$c(h)\\Omega=0$. The annihilation maps are conjugate-linear in $h$; the\ncreation maps are linear in $h$. These formulas define sector maps on the\nfinite-particle subspace; their maximal closed domains and operator relations are proved in\n[[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. No physical statistics assignment or spin–statistics\ntheorem is part of these mathematical definitions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. A natural number is a von Neumann natural, that is a set,\nand $n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ ([[def-natural-numbers]],\n[[lem-nat-order-is-membership]]), so\n\n$$\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\;=\\; \\{\\, x \\;:\\; x \\text{ is a function } n \\to \\mathbb{R} \\,\\}$$\n\nis the function space of [[def-function-space]] at $F = \\mathbb{R}$ and $X = n$,\na vector space over $\\mathbb{R}$ under the pointwise operations\n([[def-vector-space]]). We write $x_k := x(k)$ for $k < n$, and two elements of\n$\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ are equal exactly when they agree at every $k < n$. This is the\nsame set that [[lem-metrics-on-rn]] calls $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$.\n\nThe **Euclidean inner product** of $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is the real number\n\n$$\\langle x, y\\rangle \\;:=\\; \\sum_{k<n} x_k\\,y_k ,$$\n\nthe finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]] applied to the list $k \\mapsto x_k y_k$\n(extended by $0$ beyond $n$, as every finite list in this library is). The\n**Euclidean norm** of $x$ is\n\n$$\\lVert x\\rVert_2 \\;:=\\; \\sqrt{\\langle x, x\\rangle},$$\n\nwhich is defined because $\\langle x,x\\rangle = \\sum_{k<n} x_k^{2} \\ge 0$ (a sum\nof nonnegative terms, [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 4 and\n[[lem-of-square-positive]], the case $x_k = 0$ giving $x_k^2 = 0$ by\n[[def-integer-power]]) and every nonnegative real has a unique nonnegative square\nroot ([[thm-of-square-roots]]).\n\n### Both are defined for every $n$, including $n = 0$\n\nAt $n = 0$ the set $\\mathbb{R}^{0}$ has exactly one element, the empty function,\nand it is the zero vector space ([[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]] clause 5); the sum\nabove is the empty sum, so $\\langle x,y\\rangle = 0$ and $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = 0$.\n**This is the first place on this page where the two index regimes diverge, and\nthe divergence is deliberate.** The published metrics $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ of\n[[lem-metrics-on-rn]] are defined only for $n \\ge 1$, because $d_\\infty$ would\notherwise be a maximum over the empty index set; the algebra above needs no such\nrestriction. The boundary in this page runs between the algebra and the metric,\nnot where a reader would guess, and [[rem-rn-conventions-and-scope]] lists\nexactly which items inherit $n \\ge 1$.\n\n### The algebra of the inner product\n\nFor all $x, y, z \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ and $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}$:\n\n1. **Symmetry.** $\\langle x,y\\rangle = \\langle y,x\\rangle$, since $x_ky_k = y_kx_k$\n   termwise.\n2. **Additivity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle x + y, z\\rangle = \\langle x,z\\rangle + \\langle y,z\\rangle$: the list\n   $k \\mapsto (x_k+y_k)z_k$ is the termwise sum of $k \\mapsto x_kz_k$ and\n   $k \\mapsto y_kz_k$, so [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 1 applies.\n3. **Homogeneity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle \\lambda x, y\\rangle = \\lambda\\langle x,y\\rangle$, by\n   [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 2.\n4. **Bilinearity.** Clauses 2 and 3 together with symmetry give the same two laws\n   in the second argument.\n5. **Positive definiteness.** $\\langle x,x\\rangle \\ge 0$, and\n   $\\langle x,x\\rangle = 0$ if and only if $x = 0$. Indeed a vanishing sum of\n   nonnegative terms has every term $0$ ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 4), so\n   $x_k^{2} = 0$ for every $k < n$, and a nonzero real has a positive square\n   ([[lem-of-square-positive]]), whence $x_k = 0$ for every $k < n$ and $x = 0$.\n6. **Agreement with the published Euclidean metric.** For $n \\ge 1$ and\n   $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$,\n   $\\lVert x - y\\rVert_2 = \\sqrt{\\sum_{k<n}(x_k-y_k)^{2}} = d_2(x,y)$, the two\n   sides being the same expression ([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). In particular\n   $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = d_2(x, 0)$.\n\nThat $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ is a norm in the sense of [[def-norm-and-normed-space]]\nis proved in [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]], where the triangle\ninequality is obtained from the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality; it is not assumed\nhere.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For each $\\mathbf k\\ne0$, $n^Tn=n\\cdot n=1$ by [F2], so $P_T^*=P_T$ and\n$$P_T^2=(I-nn^T)^2=I-2nn^T+n(n^Tn)n^T=I-nn^T=P_T.$$\nIts kernel is $\\operatorname{span}\\{n\\}$ and its range is $n^\\perp$, of dimension two. Its operator norm is one. The arbitrary value at the single point $\\mathbf k=0$ does not affect its multiplication operator on $L^2$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Under [A2], [A1] makes each complex scalar $L^2$ component complete for its first-variable-linear pairing $(u,v)_1=\\int u\\overline v$. The three-component direct sum is complete componentwise. Its second-variable-linear pairing is $\\langle u,v\\rangle_2:=(v,u)_1=\\int\\overline u\\cdot v$, with the same norm; [F1] fixes this convention. Thus $\\mathcal H$ is complete. The multiplier $M_{P_T}$ is bounded and idempotent by step 1.1. If $u_r=M_{P_T}v_r\\to u$ in $\\mathcal H$, then $M_{P_T}u=\\lim_rM_{P_T}u_r=\\lim_ru_r=u$ by boundedness, so $u$ belongs to its range. Thus $\\mathcal H_T$ is closed and inherits a positive Hilbert inner product. Since $P_Tu=u$ is equivalent to $n\\cdot u=0$ for unit $n$ by [F2], the pointwise range formula gives the displayed transverse condition almost everywhere.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "given"
          ]
        }
      ],
