    "lem-qft-photon-null-quotient": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-photon-transverse-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let\n$$\\mathcal H=L^2(\\mathbb R^3,d^3\\mathbf k;\\mathbb C^3),\\qquad\n\\langle u,v\\rangle_2=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\overline{u(\\mathbf k)}\\cdot v(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k,$$\nwith the second-variable-linear convention of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]; the dot product and norm on each $\\mathbb R^3$ fiber are those of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], and Hilbert completeness follows from [[lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space]]. For $\\mathbf k\\ne0$, write $n(\\mathbf k)=\\mathbf k/|\\mathbf k|$ and\n$$P_T(\\mathbf k)=I_3-n(\\mathbf k)n(\\mathbf k)^T.$$\nAssign any value at $\\mathbf k=0$, which is a Lebesgue-null set. Let $M_{P_T}$ be multiplication by this measurable matrix field and define the positive transverse photon space\n$$\\mathcal H_T=\\operatorname{Ran}M_{P_T}\n=\\{u\\in\\mathcal H:\\mathbf k\\cdot u(\\mathbf k)=0\\text{ for almost every }\\mathbf k\\ne0\\}.$$\nThe inner product on $\\mathcal H_T$ is the restriction of that on $\\mathcal H$. Each nonzero momentum fiber is the two-dimensional plane perpendicular to $\\mathbf k$; no global continuous polarization frame is part of the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The map $Q:\\mathcal K\\to L^2(\\mathbb R^3)$ defined by $Q\\epsilon=-\\epsilon^0+n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\epsilon$ is bounded because $|Q\\epsilon|^2\\le2(|\\epsilon^0|^2+|\\boldsymbol\\epsilon|^2)$ pointwise. Thus $\\mathcal K_0=\\ker Q$ is closed. For $\\epsilon\\in\\mathcal K_0$, $n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\epsilon=\\epsilon^0$, so $n\\cdot T\\epsilon=0$ and $T\\epsilon\\in\\mathcal H_T$ by [F1]. Also $\\|T\\epsilon\\|_2\\le\\|\\boldsymbol\\epsilon\\|_2+\\|\\epsilon^0\\|_2$, proving boundedness.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Write $\\boldsymbol\\epsilon=T\\epsilon+n\\epsilon^0$ and $\\boldsymbol\\delta=T\\delta+n\\delta^0$. The cross terms in $\\boldsymbol\\epsilon^\\dagger\\boldsymbol\\delta$ vanish because each $T$ vector is orthogonal to $n$, while the remaining $n$ term is $\\overline{\\epsilon^0}\\delta^0$ and cancels the time component $-\\overline{\\epsilon^0}\\delta^0$ of $\\eta$. Hence $[\\epsilon,\\delta]_\\eta=\\langle T\\epsilon,T\\delta\\rangle_2$. Given any $u\\in\\mathcal H_T$, $\\epsilon=(0,u)$ belongs to $\\mathcal K_0$ and $T\\epsilon=u$, so $T$ is onto.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The equation $T\\epsilon=0$ is equivalent to $\\boldsymbol\\epsilon=n\\epsilon^0$, so $\\epsilon=b(1,n)$ with $b=\\epsilon^0\\in L^2$; each such vector is in $\\mathcal K_0$. Step 1.2 shows these vectors pair to zero with every member of $\\mathcal K_0$. Conversely, if $\\epsilon$ pairs to zero with all of $\\mathcal K_0$, choose $\\delta=(0,T\\epsilon)$, which lies in $\\mathcal K_0$ by [F1]; then $0=[\\epsilon,\\delta]_\\eta=\\|T\\epsilon\\|_2^2$, so $T\\epsilon=0$. Thus the radical is exactly $\\mathcal N$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.2",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "The induced map $\\mathcal K_0/\\mathcal N\\to\\mathcal H_T$, $[\\epsilon]\\mapsto T\\epsilon$, is well-defined, injective by the kernel calculation, onto by step 1.2, and preserves the inner product by that same step. It is therefore an isometry onto the complete Hilbert space $\\mathcal H_T$, proving positivity and completeness of the quotient.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1",
            "given"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement does not assert a case about an empty set or empty region; its objects and quantifiers are stated explicitly."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 2.1 includes b=0 and identifies the zero class."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No one-element classification is asserted in this item."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 2.1 identifies the full degenerate radical as ker T, including every null vector b(1,n)."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No interval endpoint or boundary-value case is asserted in this item."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement explicitly carries Countable Choice; no family is selected silently in the local argument."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The item does not assert an if-and-only-if statement, so there is no separate forward direction."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The item does not assert an if-and-only-if statement, so there is no separate reverse direction."
        }
      ]
    },
    "lem-qft-photon-smeared-potential": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-photon-transverse-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let\n$$\\mathcal H=L^2(\\mathbb R^3,d^3\\mathbf k;\\mathbb C^3),\\qquad\n\\langle u,v\\rangle_2=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\overline{u(\\mathbf k)}\\cdot v(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k,$$\nwith the second-variable-linear convention of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]; the dot product and norm on each $\\mathbb R^3$ fiber are those of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], and Hilbert completeness follows from [[lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space]]. For $\\mathbf k\\ne0$, write $n(\\mathbf k)=\\mathbf k/|\\mathbf k|$ and\n$$P_T(\\mathbf k)=I_3-n(\\mathbf k)n(\\mathbf k)^T.$$\nAssign any value at $\\mathbf k=0$, which is a Lebesgue-null set. Let $M_{P_T}$ be multiplication by this measurable matrix field and define the positive transverse photon space\n$$\\mathcal H_T=\\operatorname{Ran}M_{P_T}\n=\\{u\\in\\mathcal H:\\mathbf k\\cdot u(\\mathbf k)=0\\text{ for almost every }\\mathbf k\\ne0\\}.$$\nThe inner product on $\\mathcal H_T$ is the restriction of that on $\\mathcal H$. Each nonzero momentum fiber is the two-dimensional plane perpendicular to $\\mathbf k$; no global continuous polarization frame is part of the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]] and let $H$ be a separable\ncomplex Hilbert space with the second-variable-linear convention of\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. On $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ define the bosonic\ncreation and annihilation maps $a^\\dagger(h)$ and $a(h)$ and the fermionic maps\n$c^\\dagger(h)$ and $c(h)$ as in\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. Write $a_n(h)$ and $a_n^\\dagger(h)$ for\ntheir restrictions to the $n$th bosonic sector. For bosons set\n\n$$D(a(h))=\\{\\Psi\\in\\mathcal F_s(H):\\sum_{n\\ge1}\\|a_n(h)\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\},\\qquad D(a^\\dagger(h))=\\{\\Psi\\in\\mathcal F_s(H):\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\|a_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}.$$\n\nThen $\\mathcal F_s(H)$ and $\\mathcal F_a(H)$ are Hilbert spaces,\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is dense, and it is a graph core for both maximal\nbosonic sector maps. The bosonic operators on their displayed domains are\nclosed and mutually adjoint, and on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ satisfy\n\n$$[a(h),a^\\dagger(k)]=\\langle h,k\\rangle_2I,\\qquad [a(h),a(k)]=[a^\\dagger(h),a^\\dagger(k)]=0.$$\n\nThe fermionic $c(h)$ and $c^\\dagger(h)$ extend to bounded operators on the\nwhole Fock space, with norms $\\|h\\|$, and satisfy on the whole space\n\n$$\\{c(h),c^\\dagger(k)\\}=\\langle h,k\\rangle_2I,\\qquad \\{c(h),c(k)\\}=\\{c^\\dagger(h),c^\\dagger(k)\\}=0.$$\n\nThis is a mathematical construction of the two Fock representations; it does\nnot assign particle statistics to a physical species.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For an integer $d\\ge1$, let $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$ be the complex Schwartz space with seminorms\n$p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)=\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb R^d}|x^\\alpha\\partial^\\beta f(x)|$\nfor all multi-indices $\\alpha,\\beta$; this is exactly\n[[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]]. Let\n$\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^d)=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d;\\mathbb C)$ with the support convention of\n[[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]] and the locally convex LF topology of\n[[def-test-function-topology]]. The tempered-distribution space $\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^d)$\nis the space of continuous complex-linear functionals on this topological\n$\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^d)$, paired bilinearly with test functions, as in\n[[def-tempered-distribution]].\n\nFor every complex Hilbert space $H$, use the second-variable-linear pairing\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_2:=(v,u)_1$, where $(\\cdot,\\cdot)_1$ is the first-variable-linear\nHilbert pairing of [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]. Thus\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_2$ is linear in $v$ and conjugate-linear in $u$.\nFor each tensor product, use the same norm completion as\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction]], but equip it with the\nsecond-variable-linear pairing obtained by conjugating that supplier's\nfirst-variable-linear pairing. This leaves the norm and completion unchanged;\non elementary tensors it gives the product of the second-variable pairings.\nLet $H^{\\widehat\\otimes n}$ be the iterated Hilbert tensor completion, with\n$H^{\\widehat\\otimes0}:=\\mathbb C$; the tensor inner product is the product of\nthe factor pairings. For $n\\ge1$, let\n$P_{s,n}=n!^{-1}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}U_\\pi$ and\n$P_{a,n}=n!^{-1}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\pi)U_\\pi$, using the\npermutation action and the orthogonal projections of\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections]]. Define\n$H_s^{(n)}=\\operatorname{ran}P_{s,n}$ and\n$H_a^{(n)}=\\operatorname{ran}P_{a,n}$, with\n$H_s^{(0)}=H_a^{(0)}=\\mathbb C$. These are the symmetric and antisymmetric\n$n$-particle sectors.\n\nThe bosonic and fermionic Fock Hilbert spaces are the square-summable direct\nsums\n$\\mathcal F_s(H)=\\{(\\Psi_n)_{n\\ge0}:\\Psi_n\\in H_s^{(n)},\\\n\\sum_n\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}$ and\n$\\mathcal F_a(H)=\\{(\\Psi_n)_{n\\ge0}:\\Psi_n\\in H_a^{(n)},\\\n\\sum_n\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}$, with the componentwise inner product.\nTheir vacuum is $\\Omega=(1,0,0,\\ldots)$. The finite-particle subspace\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ consists of sequences with only finitely many nonzero\ncomponents.\n\nFor $n\\ge0$, bosonic creation on sector $n$ is\n$a_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt{n+1}P_{s,n+1}(h\\otimes\\Psi_n)$; for $n\\ge1$,\nbosonic annihilation is\n$a_n(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt n(C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)})\\Psi_n$, where\n$C_h(v)=\\langle h,v\\rangle_2$ and its tensor extension is the bounded map of\n[[lem-nrqm-composites-bounded-tensor-maps]], and $a(h)\\Omega=0$. Define fermionic creation\nby $c_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt{n+1}P_{a,n+1}(h\\otimes\\Psi_n)$; for $n\\ge1$,\nfermionic annihilation is the corresponding first-factor contraction\n$c_n(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt n(C_h\\otimes I^{\\otimes(n-1)})\\Psi_n$, and\n$c(h)\\Omega=0$. The annihilation maps are conjugate-linear in $h$; the\ncreation maps are linear in $h$. These formulas define sector maps on the\nfinite-particle subspace; their maximal closed domains and operator relations are proved in\n[[lem-qft-fock-ccr-car]]. No physical statistics assignment or spin–statistics\ntheorem is part of these mathematical definitions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The integral in $g_j$ is the spacetime Fourier transform of a compactly supported smooth function, hence decreases faster than every inverse power of $|(\\omega,\\mathbf k)|$ by repeated integration by parts. Near $\\mathbf k=0$, it is bounded by $\\int|j|$, while the squared prefactor is $1/(2\\omega)=1/(2|\\mathbf k|)$. The radial integral of this bound is a constant times $\\int_0^1r^2/r\\,dr<\\infty$. At infinity the rapid Fourier decay and $\\|P_T\\|\\le1$ make the squared norm integrable. Also $P_Tg_j=g_j$ almost everywhere. Therefore $g_j\\in\\mathcal H_T$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "By [F3], a vector in $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ has only finitely many nonzero sectors. On each such sector [F2] bounds creation and annihilation by the corresponding square-root particle number times $\\|g_j\\|\\|\\Psi_n\\|$. Thus both maps and their sum are defined on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. The same estimates bound every matrix element between fixed finite-particle vectors by a constant times $\\|g_j\\|$. On each fixed compact support, repeated integration by parts in step 1.1 bounds $\\|g_j\\|$ by finitely many smooth-test seminorms, so these matrix elements are continuous in the test-function topology. This proves the asserted distributional smearing without defining a point field.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Apply the CCR [F2] to the positive- and negative-frequency terms of the mode expansion and use $E=-\\partial_0A$. At equal time the two cross terms combine to\n$$-i\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}\n\\left(\\delta_{ij}-\\frac{k_i k_j}{|\\mathbf k|^2}\\right)e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot(\\mathbf x-\\mathbf y)}.$$\nThis is the distributional Fourier multiplier $-iP_{T,ij}(-i\\nabla_{\\mathbf x})\\delta^3(\\mathbf x-\\mathbf y)$; its action is defined by pairing the bounded multiplier against Schwartz tests. Thus the equal-time commutator holds after smearing, as claimed.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.2",
            "given"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement does not assert a case about an empty set or empty region; its objects and quantifiers are stated explicitly."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 1.1 gives g_j=0 for the zero test, so the smeared field is zero."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No one-element classification is asserted in this item."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 1.1 proves the 1/|k| squared-norm bound is integrable at k=0."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
