    "lem-qft-photon-transverse-projector-kernel": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-minus-laplacian-of-the-fundamental-solution-is-dirac",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and let $n\\ge2$. The locally integrable kernel $\\Phi$ of [[def-laplace-fundamental-solution-with-positive-minus-laplacian-sign]] defines a regular distribution $T_\\Phi\\in\\mathcal D'(\\mathbb R^n)$ and satisfies $-\\Delta T_\\Phi=\\delta_0$. For every $y\\in\\mathbb R^n$, the regular distribution associated with $x\\mapsto\\Phi(x-y)$ satisfies $-\\Delta_x T_{\\Phi(\\cdot-y)}=\\delta_y$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qft-photon-smeared-potential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $j=(j_1,j_2,j_3)\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb R^3)$ and use $\\mathcal H_T$ and $P_T$ from [[def-qft-photon-transverse-space]]. For $\\omega=|\\mathbf k|$ and $\\mathbf k\\ne0$, define\n$$g_j(\\mathbf k)=\\frac{1}{(2\\pi)^{3/2}(2\\omega)^{1/2}}\\,\nP_T(\\mathbf k)\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}j(x)e^{i\\omega x^0-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\,d^4x.$$\nThen $g_j\\in\\mathcal H_T$. On the finite-particle domain $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ of the bosonic Fock space $\\mathcal F_s(\\mathcal H_T)$, the smeared transverse potential is\n$$A_T(j)=a(g_j)+a^\\dagger(g_j).$$\nIts matrix elements are continuous in the test-function topology; it is an operator-valued distribution and does not assign a pointwise operator to $A_i(x)$. With $E_i=-\\partial_0A_i$, the equal-time canonical commutator is the distribution\n$$[A_i(0,\\mathbf x),E_j(0,\\mathbf y)]\n=-i\\,P_{T,ij}(-i\\nabla_{\\mathbf x})\\delta^3(\\mathbf x-\\mathbf y).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $n \\ge 1$,\nlet $S^{n-1} \\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ be the unit sphere, and let $\\sigma$ be the\nset function of [[def-polar-surface-measure-on-the-unit-sphere]]. Then $\\sigma$\nis a finite Borel measure on $S^{n-1}$, and for every Borel measurable\n$f : \\mathbb R^n \\to [0,\\infty]$,\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^n} f(x)\\,d\\lambda_n(x) = \\int_0^\\infty \\int_{S^{n-1}} f(r\\omega) r^{n-1}\\,d\\sigma(\\omega)\\,dr.$$\nMoreover, $\\sigma$ is the unique Borel measure on $S^{n-1}$ with this\nproperty.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-em-sphere-zonal-moments",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ($\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$), as carried by the surface-measure interface below ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $\\sigma$ be the polar surface measure on the unit sphere $S^2\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ ([[def-polar-surface-measure-on-the-unit-sphere]]), let $e_3:=(0,0,1)$ and write $n_3=n\\cdot e_3$ for $n\\in S^2$. Then:\n\n(a) [Zonal identity] for every continuous $f:[-1,1]\\to\\mathbb R$, $$\\int_{S^2}f(n_3)\\,d\\sigma(n)=2\\pi\\int_{-1}^{1}f(u)\\,du.$$\n\n(b) [Linear weighted moment] for every continuous $f$ and every $a\\in\\mathbb R^3$, $$\\int_{S^2}(n\\cdot a)\\,f(n_3)\\,d\\sigma(n)=2\\pi\\,a_3\\int_{-1}^{1}u\\,f(u)\\,du.$$\n\n(c) [Quadratic weighted moment] for every continuous $f$ and every $a\\in\\mathbb R^3$, $$\\int_{S^2}(n\\cdot a)^2f(n_3)\\,d\\sigma(n)=2\\pi\\,a_3^{2}\\int_{-1}^{1}u^{2}f(u)\\,du+\\pi\\,(a_1^{2}+a_2^{2})\\int_{-1}^{1}(1-u^{2})f(u)\\,du.$$\n\n(d) [Moments] $\\sigma(S^2)=4\\pi$, $\\int_{S^2}n\\,d\\sigma=0$, $\\int_{S^2}n_in_j\\,d\\sigma=(4\\pi/3)\\delta_{ij}$, and for every $a\\in\\mathbb R^3$ $$\\int_{S^2}(n\\cdot a)^2d\\sigma=\\frac{4\\pi}{3}|a|^2,\\qquad \\int_{S^2}|n\\times a|^2d\\sigma=\\frac{8\\pi}{3}|a|^2,$$ where $\\delta_{ij}$ is the Kronecker symbol and $\\times$ the cross product of [[def-cross-product-in-r3]].\n\n(e) [Scope] (a)-(d) are identities for the polar surface measure of the unit sphere with only the stated continuity of $f$; they assert no physical field, flux or radiation statement. The measure is finite, so all displayed integrals are absolutely convergent; $f$ need not be nonnegative. For the uses on this page $f$ is a rational function with denominator bounded below by $1-b>0$, hence continuous on $[-1,1]$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and $n\\ge2$. The chart surface measure on $S^{n-1}$ equals the polar measure $\\sigma$. Orthogonal transformations preserve it. The map $\\omega\\mapsto a+R\\omega$, $R>0$, multiplies surface measure by $R^{n-1}$, and $|S^{n-1}|=n|B_1|$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The class $L^1(\\mu)$ is a complex vector space, and the Lebesgue integral is\ncomplex-linear on it:\n$$\\int (\\alpha f+\\beta g)\\,d\\mu=\\alpha\\int f\\,d\\mu+\\beta\\int g\\,d\\mu \\qquad(\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb C,\\ f,g\\in L^1(\\mu)).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand hence\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. A natural number is a von Neumann natural, that is a set,\nand $n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ ([[def-natural-numbers]],\n[[lem-nat-order-is-membership]]), so\n\n$$\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\;=\\; \\{\\, x \\;:\\; x \\text{ is a function } n \\to \\mathbb{R} \\,\\}$$\n\nis the function space of [[def-function-space]] at $F = \\mathbb{R}$ and $X = n$,\na vector space over $\\mathbb{R}$ under the pointwise operations\n([[def-vector-space]]). We write $x_k := x(k)$ for $k < n$, and two elements of\n$\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ are equal exactly when they agree at every $k < n$. This is the\nsame set that [[lem-metrics-on-rn]] calls $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$.\n\nThe **Euclidean inner product** of $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is the real number\n\n$$\\langle x, y\\rangle \\;:=\\; \\sum_{k<n} x_k\\,y_k ,$$\n\nthe finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]] applied to the list $k \\mapsto x_k y_k$\n(extended by $0$ beyond $n$, as every finite list in this library is). The\n**Euclidean norm** of $x$ is\n\n$$\\lVert x\\rVert_2 \\;:=\\; \\sqrt{\\langle x, x\\rangle},$$\n\nwhich is defined because $\\langle x,x\\rangle = \\sum_{k<n} x_k^{2} \\ge 0$ (a sum\nof nonnegative terms, [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 4 and\n[[lem-of-square-positive]], the case $x_k = 0$ giving $x_k^2 = 0$ by\n[[def-integer-power]]) and every nonnegative real has a unique nonnegative square\nroot ([[thm-of-square-roots]]).\n\n### Both are defined for every $n$, including $n = 0$\n\nAt $n = 0$ the set $\\mathbb{R}^{0}$ has exactly one element, the empty function,\nand it is the zero vector space ([[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]] clause 5); the sum\nabove is the empty sum, so $\\langle x,y\\rangle = 0$ and $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = 0$.\n**This is the first place on this page where the two index regimes diverge, and\nthe divergence is deliberate.** The published metrics $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ of\n[[lem-metrics-on-rn]] are defined only for $n \\ge 1$, because $d_\\infty$ would\notherwise be a maximum over the empty index set; the algebra above needs no such\nrestriction. The boundary in this page runs between the algebra and the metric,\nnot where a reader would guess, and [[rem-rn-conventions-and-scope]] lists\nexactly which items inherit $n \\ge 1$.\n\n### The algebra of the inner product\n\nFor all $x, y, z \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ and $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}$:\n\n1. **Symmetry.** $\\langle x,y\\rangle = \\langle y,x\\rangle$, since $x_ky_k = y_kx_k$\n   termwise.\n2. **Additivity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle x + y, z\\rangle = \\langle x,z\\rangle + \\langle y,z\\rangle$: the list\n   $k \\mapsto (x_k+y_k)z_k$ is the termwise sum of $k \\mapsto x_kz_k$ and\n   $k \\mapsto y_kz_k$, so [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 1 applies.\n3. **Homogeneity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle \\lambda x, y\\rangle = \\lambda\\langle x,y\\rangle$, by\n   [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 2.\n4. **Bilinearity.** Clauses 2 and 3 together with symmetry give the same two laws\n   in the second argument.\n5. **Positive definiteness.** $\\langle x,x\\rangle \\ge 0$, and\n   $\\langle x,x\\rangle = 0$ if and only if $x = 0$. Indeed a vanishing sum of\n   nonnegative terms has every term $0$ ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 4), so\n   $x_k^{2} = 0$ for every $k < n$, and a nonzero real has a positive square\n   ([[lem-of-square-positive]]), whence $x_k = 0$ for every $k < n$ and $x = 0$.\n6. **Agreement with the published Euclidean metric.** For $n \\ge 1$ and\n   $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$,\n   $\\lVert x - y\\rVert_2 = \\sqrt{\\sum_{k<n}(x_k-y_k)^{2}} = d_2(x,y)$, the two\n   sides being the same expression ([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). In particular\n   $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = d_2(x, 0)$.\n\nThat $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ is a norm in the sense of [[def-norm-and-normed-space]]\nis proved in [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]], where the triangle\ninequality is obtained from the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality; it is not assumed\nhere.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $n \\ge 1$,\nlet $S^{n-1} \\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ be the unit sphere, and let $\\sigma$ be the\nset function of [[def-polar-surface-measure-on-the-unit-sphere]]. Then $\\sigma$\nis a finite Borel measure on $S^{n-1}$, and for every Borel measurable\n$f : \\mathbb R^n \\to [0,\\infty]$,\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^n} f(x)\\,d\\lambda_n(x) = \\int_0^\\infty \\int_{S^{n-1}} f(r\\omega) r^{n-1}\\,d\\sigma(\\omega)\\,dr.$$\nMoreover, $\\sigma$ is the unique Borel measure on $S^{n-1}$ with this\nproperty.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $a>0$, the function $x\\mapsto a^x$ is continuous on $\\mathbb R$ and\n$$(a^x)'=a^x\\log a.$$\nFor $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R$, the function $x\\mapsto x^\\alpha$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty)$, with\n$$(x^\\alpha)'=\\alpha x^{\\alpha-1}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g[A] \\subseteq B$ and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of\n$A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) at which $g$ is differentiable\n([[def-derivative]]), put $b := g(c)$, and suppose $b$ is a limit point of $B$\nat which $f$ is differentiable. Then $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $c$ and\n\n$$(f \\circ g)'(c) \\;=\\; f'\\bigl(g(c)\\bigr)\\,g'(c) .$$\n\n**Both limit-point hypotheses are needed, and neither is automatic.** That $c$\nis a limit point of $A$ is what makes $g'(c)$ and $(f \\circ g)'(c)$ defined\nsymbols; that $b = g(c)$ is a limit point of $B$ is what makes $f'(b)$ one.\nNothing forces the second: $g$ may be differentiable at $c$ and send $c$ to an\nisolated point of $B$, and there $f'(b)$ is not defined and the formula asserts\nnothing.\n\n**No case analysis appears anywhere.** The naive difference-quotient proof\nwrites $\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(c))}{g(x) - g(c)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(c)}{x - c}$\nand then has to say what happens where $g(x) = g(c)$, which may occur at points\narbitrarily close to $c$. Carathéodory's factorisation never divides by the\ninner increment, so the difficulty does not arise.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of $A$\n([[def-limit-point-r]]), let $f, g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at $c$\n([[def-derivative]]) and let $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then:\n\n1. $f + g$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(f+g)'(c) = f'(c) + g'(c)$;\n2. $\\alpha f$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(\\alpha f)'(c) = \\alpha f'(c)$;\n3. $fg$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(fg)'(c) = f'(c)g(c) + f(c)g'(c)$;\n4. if $g(c) \\ne 0$ then, writing $A_0 := \\{\\, x \\in A : g(x) \\ne 0 \\,\\}$, the\n   point $c$ lies in $A_0$ and is a limit point of $A_0$, the quotient\n   $(f/g)|_{A_0} : A_0 \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $x \\mapsto f(x)/g(x)$, is differentiable\n   at $c$ as a function on $A_0$, and\n   $$\\bigl((f/g)|_{A_0}\\bigr)'(c) \\;=\\; \\frac{f'(c)\\,g(c) - f(c)\\,g'(c)}{g(c)^{2}} .$$\n\nEach claim asserts two things: that the derivative on the left exists, and that\nit has the stated value. Both are proved.\n\n**Why claim 4 is stated on $A_0$.** The function $f/g$ is not defined where $g$\nvanishes, and $g$ may vanish at points of $A$ far from $c$; restricting to $A_0$\nis forced. That the restriction still has $c$ as a limit point, so that a\nderivative there means anything at all, is not free either, and it is the last\nclaim of [[lem-sign-preservation-near-a-limit]] applied to $g$. The hypothesis\nis $g(c) \\ne 0$, not \"$g$ vanishes nowhere\".\n\n**Everything is proved through\n[[thm-caratheodory-characterisation]].** No difference quotient is estimated and\nno limit theorem beyond continuity is used, so no choice principle is spent.\nThe four identities are four algebraic rearrangements of an increment, each\nfollowed by a reading of [[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb C$ belong to\n$L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$. Then:\n\n1. for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, the section $f_x$ belongs to $L^1(\\nu)$;\n2. for $\\nu$-almost every $y$, the section $f^y$ belongs to $L^1(\\mu)$;\n3. after assigning the value $0$ on the exceptional parameter sets, the\n   section-integral functions are integrable; and\n4. the three integrals agree:\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, with $n\\ge1$. A complex-valued function is smooth if its real and imaginary parts are $C^k$ for every finite $k$, in the sense of [[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]. Coordinate derivatives act on these two parts separately. We write $\\partial^\\alpha$ for the multi-index convention there, and $\\partial^0\\varphi=\\varphi$.\n\nThe **test-function space** is $\\mathcal D(\\Omega)=C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb C)$: its members are smooth functions whose support, the closure in $\\Omega$ of their nonzero set, is a compact subset of $\\Omega$. Equivalently, the zero extension to $\\mathbb R^n$ is smooth with compact support contained in $\\Omega$: compactness gives a neighborhood of every boundary point disjoint from the support, where that extension vanishes. Functions are actual functions, not equivalence classes.\n\nPointwise addition and scalar multiplication make this a complex vector space; the support of a sum lies in the union of the two compact supports. The zero function has empty support. If $\\Omega=\\varnothing$ there is just the empty function, which is the zero vector. The topology is specified in subsequent definitions. Distribution pairings throughout this page are complex bilinear, with no conjugation on test functions. One may restrict all constructions to real scalars.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For $\\varepsilon>0$, $m_\\varepsilon(\\mathbf k)=e^{-\\varepsilon|\\mathbf k|}/|\\mathbf k|^2$ is integrable: [F3] and $\\sigma(S^2)=4\\pi$ give $\\int|m_\\varepsilon|d^3\\mathbf k=4\\pi\\int_0^\\infty e^{-\\varepsilon s}ds<\\infty$. Its inverse Fourier integral is radial. Rotate the polar axis to $\\mathbf x$ using [F4], and apply the zonal identity separately to the real and imaginary parts of $u\\mapsto e^{is|\\mathbf x|u}$. The polar formula [F3] is stated for nonnegative Borel functions; since this complex integrand is absolutely integrable, apply it separately to the positive and negative parts of its real and imaginary parts and recombine by [F5]. The angular integral is $4\\pi\\sin(s r)/(s r)$, where $r=|\\mathbf x|$. Thus\n$$G_\\varepsilon(r)=(2\\pi)^{-3}\\int e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}m_\\varepsilon(\\mathbf k)d^3\\mathbf k\n=\\frac1{2\\pi^2r}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-\\varepsilon s}\\frac{\\sin(sr)}s\\,ds.$$\nSince $\\sin(sr)/s=\\int_0^r\\cos(st)dt$ and $\\int_0^r\\int_0^\\infty e^{-\\varepsilon s}|\\cos(st)|dsdt\\le r/\\varepsilon$, Fubini [F7] applies. The real part of $\\int_0^\\infty e^{-(\\varepsilon-it)s}ds=(\\varepsilon-it)^{-1}$ is $\\varepsilon/(\\varepsilon^2+t^2)$; integrating it over $t\\in[0,r]$ gives $\\arctan(r/\\varepsilon)$. Therefore\n$$G_\\varepsilon(r)=\\frac{\\arctan(r/\\varepsilon)}{2\\pi^2r}.$$",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F7",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "For every test $\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$ [F8], the bound $|G_\\varepsilon(\\mathbf x)|\\le(4\\pi|\\mathbf x|)^{-1}$ and [F3] make $G_\\varepsilon\\varphi$ dominated by an integrable function; [F5] gives the distributional limit $G_\\varepsilon\\to G$. To identify the same limit in Fourier variables, repeated integration by parts in a coordinate with $|k_j|\\ge|\\mathbf k|/\\sqrt3$ gives $|\\widehat\\varphi(\\mathbf k)|\\le C_N(1+|\\mathbf k|)^{-N}$ for every integer $N$, while $|\\widehat\\varphi|\\le\\|\\varphi\\|_1$ near zero. Hence $|\\mathbf k|^{-2}\\widehat\\varphi(-\\mathbf k)$ is integrable by [F3]. Fubini [F7] identifies $\\int G_\\varepsilon\\varphi$ with $(2\\pi)^{-3}\\int m_\\varepsilon(\\mathbf k)\\widehat\\varphi(-\\mathbf k)d^3\\mathbf k$. Since $|m_\\varepsilon|\\le|\\mathbf k|^{-2}$, [F5] shows this tends to the inverse Fourier pairing with $|\\mathbf k|^{-2}$. Both limits are therefore the same distribution $G$, proving $\\mathcal F^{-1}(|\\mathbf k|^{-2})=G$. By [F1] this is the published normalized fundamental solution.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "step 1.1",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Integration by parts against compact smooth tests gives Fourier multiplier $-k_i k_j$ for $\\partial_i\\partial_jG$. Step 1.2 therefore gives\n$$\\mathcal F^{-1}\\!\\left(\\delta_{ij}-\\frac{k_ik_j}{|\\mathbf k|^2}\\right)\n=\\delta_{ij}\\delta^3+\\partial_i\\partial_jG.$$\nFor $\\mathbf x\\ne0$, put $s=|\\mathbf x|^2$ by [F6], so $G=(4\\pi)^{-1}s^{-1/2}$. The power, chain and sum rules [F7] give\n$$\\partial_iG=-\\frac{x_i}{4\\pi s^{3/2}},\\qquad\n\\partial_i\\partial_jG=-\\frac{\\delta_{ij}}{4\\pi r^3}+\\frac{3x_ix_j}{4\\pi r^5}.$$\nSubstitution into [F2] proves the stated equal-time commutator kernel.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "step 1.2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
