    "lem-qft-qed-coulomb-constraint": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-qft-qed-gauge-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use smooth fields on Minkowski coordinate space with $x^0=ct$ and $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. Components of $x$ are measured in metres. Fix the Dirac matrices and adjoint of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], so $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$ and $\\bar\\psi=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta$.\n\nFor the SI-normalization bookkeeping, let $c>0$ (m s$^{-1}$), $\\hbar>0$ (J s), $\\mu_0>0$ (N A$^{-2}$), a real charge parameter $q$ (C), and a mass parameter $m>0$ (kg) be symbolic inputs; put $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$. In the current SI, $c$ and $h$ are defining constants while $\\mu_0$ is experimentally determined; the formula below keeps $\\mu_0$ symbolic and assigns it no numerical value. Use the lower-potential convention\n$$A_a=(-\\phi/c,\\mathbf A),\\qquad F_{ab}=\\partial_aA_b-\\partial_bA_a,$$\nwhere $A_a$ has units T m, $\\phi$ is in volts, and $\\mathbf A$ is in T m. Define\n$$S_0=\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c},\\qquad a_a=A_a/S_0,\\qquad f_{ab}=\\partial_aa_b-\\partial_ba_a,$$\n$$\\mu=mc/\\hbar,\\qquad g=q\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}=q/\\sqrt{\\epsilon_0\\hbar c},\\qquad D_a=\\partial_a-ig a_a.$$\nThus $a_a$ and $\\mu$ have units m$^{-1}$, $f_{ab}$ has units m$^{-2}$, and $g$ is dimensionless. For $\\psi$ with units m$^{-3/2}$, both $-\\tfrac14 f_{ab}f^{ab}$ and $\\bar\\psi(i\\gamma^aD_a-\\mu)\\psi$ have units m$^{-4}$; their integral over $d^4x$ is dimensionless and represents the action divided by $\\hbar$ in this normalization.\n\nFor a real smooth gauge parameter $\\chi$ in webers, put $\\alpha=\\chi/S_0$ (dimensionless) and use\n$$A'_a=A_a+\\partial_a\\chi,\\qquad a'_a=a_a+\\partial_a\\alpha,\\qquad \\psi'=e^{ig\\alpha}\\psi.$$\nThe induced covariant derivative is $D'_a=\\partial_a-ig a'_a$. These are formal mathematical conventions for the later conditional deductions. They do not adopt a measured value of $\\mu_0$, identify $q$ with an electron charge, impose charge quantization, or assert that interacting continuum fields exist.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $n\\ge1$\nand let $e_k(x)=\\exp(2\\pi i\\,k\\cdot x)$, $k\\in\\mathbb Z^n$, be the coordinate\ncharacters on $\\mathbb T^n$\n([[def-the-one-dimensional-torus-and-normalized-haar-integral]],\n[[def-fourier-coefficients-and-trigonometric-polynomials]]). Then:\n\n1. $(e_k)_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^n}$ is an orthonormal family in the complex Hilbert\n   space $L^2(\\mathbb T^n)$;\n2. it is an orthonormal basis: its closed linear span is $L^2(\\mathbb T^n)$;\n3. consequently the Fourier expansion\n   $f=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^n}\\widehat f(k)e_k$ and Parseval's identity\n   $$\\|f\\|_2^2=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^n}|\\widehat f(k)|^2,\\qquad \\langle f,g\\rangle=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^n}\\widehat f(k)\\overline{\\widehat g(k)}$$\n   hold for all $f,g\\in L^2(\\mathbb T^n)$, in the finite-subset-net sense; and\n4. the Fourier coefficient map is a surjective linear isometry of\n   $L^2(\\mathbb T^n)$ onto $\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z^n,\\mathbb C)$.\n\nNo Hilbert tensor-product identification is used anywhere.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Since the displayed density contains no $\\dot\\phi$, its canonical momentum is $\\pi_\\phi=\\partial\\mathcal L/\\partial\\dot\\phi=0$. Differentiating with respect to $\\dot{\\mathbf A}$ gives $\\boldsymbol\\pi_A=\\epsilon_0(\\dot{\\mathbf A}+\\nabla\\phi)$. The Legendre expression $\\boldsymbol\\pi_A\\cdot\\dot{\\mathbf A}-\\mathcal L$ equals\n$$\\frac{|\\boldsymbol\\pi_A|^2}{2\\epsilon_0}-\\boldsymbol\\pi_A\\cdot\\nabla\\phi+\n\\frac{|\\nabla\\times\\mathbf A|^2}{2\\mu_0}+\\rho\\phi-\\mathbf J\\cdot\\mathbf A.$$\nThe periodic boundary has no surface term, and integrating $-\\boldsymbol\\pi_A\\cdot\\nabla\\phi$ gives $\\phi\\,\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\pi_A$. This yields the displayed $H$; varying the scalar multiplier gives the Gauss constraint. If $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf A=0$, then $\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\pi_A=\\epsilon_0\\Delta\\phi$, so the constraint is $-\\Delta\\phi=\\rho/\\epsilon_0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Integrating this equation over the periodic box gives $0=L^{-3}\\int(-\\Delta\\phi)\\,d^3x=L^{-3}\\epsilon_0^{-1}\\int\\rho\\,d^3x$, since each periodic first derivative has equal boundary values on opposite faces. Thus neutrality is necessary. For neutral data, integrating $M$ times by parts in a coordinate with $|k_j|\\ge|k|/\\sqrt3$ gives $|\\widehat\\rho_k|\\le C_M(1+|k|)^{-M}$ for every integer $M\\ge0$; the same bound holds uniformly on compact time intervals when the data depend smoothly on time. Therefore the proposed coefficients for $\\phi$ and every differentiated Fourier series are absolutely summable: division by $|2\\pi k/L|^2$ costs only two powers and $M$ can be taken arbitrarily large.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Under Countable Choice [A2], apply [F1] after rescaling $x=Ly$: the characters $e^{2\\pi i k\\cdot x/L}$ form a complete basis for $L^{-3}d^3x$. The coefficient formula then gives $|2\\pi k/L|^2\\widehat\\phi_k=\\widehat\\rho_k/\\epsilon_0$ for $k\\ne0$ and the zero mode is zero. Absolute convergence from step 1.2 permits termwise differentiation to every order, so the resulting real smooth periodic $\\phi$ solves $-\\Delta\\phi=\\rho/\\epsilon_0$. Reality follows from $\\widehat\\rho_{-k}=\\overline{\\widehat\\rho_k}$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "step 1.2",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "If two zero-mean solutions existed, their difference $u$ would satisfy $-\\Delta u=0$ and $\\widehat u_0=0$. Parseval [F1] applied to its gradient gives $L^3\\sum_{k\\ne0}|2\\pi k/L|^2|\\widehat u_k|^2=0$, hence every coefficient vanishes; completeness then gives $u=0$. Parseval applied to $\\nabla\\phi$ and to $\\rho,\\phi$ yields\n$$\\int|\\nabla\\phi|^2=L^3\\sum_{k\\ne0}|2\\pi k/L|^2|\\widehat\\phi_k|^2,\\qquad\n\\int\\rho\\phi=L^3\\sum_{k\\ne0}\\overline{\\widehat\\rho_k}\\widehat\\phi_k.$$\nSubstituting the coefficients from step 2.1 proves the displayed positive-energy identity.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
            "given"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
