    "lem-qft-qed-dirac-first-order-constraints": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-qft-qed-gauge-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use smooth fields on Minkowski coordinate space with $x^0=ct$ and $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. Components of $x$ are measured in metres. Fix the Dirac matrices and adjoint of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], so $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$ and $\\bar\\psi=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta$.\n\nFor the SI-normalization bookkeeping, let $c>0$ (m s$^{-1}$), $\\hbar>0$ (J s), $\\mu_0>0$ (N A$^{-2}$), a real charge parameter $q$ (C), and a mass parameter $m>0$ (kg) be symbolic inputs; put $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$. In the current SI, $c$ and $h$ are defining constants while $\\mu_0$ is experimentally determined; the formula below keeps $\\mu_0$ symbolic and assigns it no numerical value. Use the lower-potential convention\n$$A_a=(-\\phi/c,\\mathbf A),\\qquad F_{ab}=\\partial_aA_b-\\partial_bA_a,$$\nwhere $A_a$ has units T m, $\\phi$ is in volts, and $\\mathbf A$ is in T m. Define\n$$S_0=\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c},\\qquad a_a=A_a/S_0,\\qquad f_{ab}=\\partial_aa_b-\\partial_ba_a,$$\n$$\\mu=mc/\\hbar,\\qquad g=q\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}=q/\\sqrt{\\epsilon_0\\hbar c},\\qquad D_a=\\partial_a-ig a_a.$$\nThus $a_a$ and $\\mu$ have units m$^{-1}$, $f_{ab}$ has units m$^{-2}$, and $g$ is dimensionless. For $\\psi$ with units m$^{-3/2}$, both $-\\tfrac14 f_{ab}f^{ab}$ and $\\bar\\psi(i\\gamma^aD_a-\\mu)\\psi$ have units m$^{-4}$; their integral over $d^4x$ is dimensionless and represents the action divided by $\\hbar$ in this normalization.\n\nFor a real smooth gauge parameter $\\chi$ in webers, put $\\alpha=\\chi/S_0$ (dimensionless) and use\n$$A'_a=A_a+\\partial_a\\chi,\\qquad a'_a=a_a+\\partial_a\\alpha,\\qquad \\psi'=e^{ig\\alpha}\\psi.$$\nThe induced covariant derivative is $D'_a=\\partial_a-ig a'_a$. These are formal mathematical conventions for the later conditional deductions. They do not adopt a measured value of $\\mu_0$, identify $q$ with an electron charge, impose charge quantization, or assert that interacting continuum fields exist.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-qed-gauge-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use smooth fields on Minkowski coordinate space with $x^0=ct$ and $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. Components of $x$ are measured in metres. Fix the Dirac matrices and adjoint of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], so $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$ and $\\bar\\psi=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta$.\n\nFor the SI-normalization bookkeeping, let $c>0$ (m s$^{-1}$), $\\hbar>0$ (J s), $\\mu_0>0$ (N A$^{-2}$), a real charge parameter $q$ (C), and a mass parameter $m>0$ (kg) be symbolic inputs; put $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$. In the current SI, $c$ and $h$ are defining constants while $\\mu_0$ is experimentally determined; the formula below keeps $\\mu_0$ symbolic and assigns it no numerical value. Use the lower-potential convention\n$$A_a=(-\\phi/c,\\mathbf A),\\qquad F_{ab}=\\partial_aA_b-\\partial_bA_a,$$\nwhere $A_a$ has units T m, $\\phi$ is in volts, and $\\mathbf A$ is in T m. Define\n$$S_0=\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c},\\qquad a_a=A_a/S_0,\\qquad f_{ab}=\\partial_aa_b-\\partial_ba_a,$$\n$$\\mu=mc/\\hbar,\\qquad g=q\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}=q/\\sqrt{\\epsilon_0\\hbar c},\\qquad D_a=\\partial_a-ig a_a.$$\nThus $a_a$ and $\\mu$ have units m$^{-1}$, $f_{ab}$ has units m$^{-2}$, and $g$ is dimensionless. For $\\psi$ with units m$^{-3/2}$, both $-\\tfrac14 f_{ab}f^{ab}$ and $\\bar\\psi(i\\gamma^aD_a-\\mu)\\psi$ have units m$^{-4}$; their integral over $d^4x$ is dimensionless and represents the action divided by $\\hbar$ in this normalization.\n\nFor a real smooth gauge parameter $\\chi$ in webers, put $\\alpha=\\chi/S_0$ (dimensionless) and use\n$$A'_a=A_a+\\partial_a\\chi,\\qquad a'_a=a_a+\\partial_a\\alpha,\\qquad \\psi'=e^{ig\\alpha}\\psi.$$\nThe induced covariant derivative is $D'_a=\\partial_a-ig a'_a$. These are formal mathematical conventions for the later conditional deductions. They do not adopt a measured value of $\\mu_0$, identify $q$ with an electron charge, impose charge quantization, or assert that interacting continuum fields exist.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Under the explicit Countable Choice assumption [A2], the canonical momenta from the displayed kinetic term are $p_j=\\partial L/\\partial\\dot z_j=i\\hbar\\bar z_j/2$ and $\\bar p_j=\\partial L/\\partial\\dot{\\bar z}_j=-i\\hbar z_j/2$. Moving each velocity-independent momentum expression to the left gives exactly $C_j=0$ and $\\bar C_j=0$. The finite canonical calculation uses the positive $\\hbar$ fixed in [F1].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "For each $j,k$, bilinearity and the elementary brackets give\n$$\\{C_j,C_k\\}=0,\\qquad\\{\\bar C_j,\\bar C_k\\}=0,\\qquad\n\\{C_j,\\bar C_k\\}=\\{p_j,(i\\hbar/2)z_k\\}+\\{-(i\\hbar/2)\\bar z_j,\\bar p_k\\}\n=-\\frac{i\\hbar}{2}\\delta_{jk}-\\frac{i\\hbar}{2}\\delta_{jk}=-i\\hbar\\delta_{jk}.$$\nAntisymmetry gives $\\{\\bar C_j,C_k\\}=i\\hbar\\delta_{jk}$, which is exactly the displayed block matrix $M$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Multiplication of the two displayed block matrices gives $MM^{-1}=M^{-1}M=I_{2N}$; each off-diagonal product is $(-i\\hbar)(i/\\hbar)=1$ or $(i\\hbar)(-i/\\hbar)=1$, and the diagonal blocks are zero. Because $\\hbar>0$, $M$ is invertible, so these primary constraints are second class by definition.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "The canonical coordinate bracket $\\{z_j,\\bar z_k\\}$ is zero. In the Dirac correction, the only nonzero factors are $\\{z_j,C_\\ell\\}=\\delta_{j\\ell}$ and $\\{\\bar C_m,\\bar z_k\\}=-\\delta_{mk}$. Hence\n$$\\{z_j,\\bar z_k\\}_D\n=0-\\delta_{j\\ell}(M^{-1})_{C_\\ell,\\bar C_m}(-\\delta_{mk})\n=(M^{-1})_{C_j,\\bar C_k}=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}\\delta_{jk}.$$\nThe same formula has no nonzero block for $\\{z_j,z_k\\}_D$ or $\\{\\bar z_j,\\bar z_k\\}_D$, so both vanish.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For a smooth $H(z,\\bar z)$, the only nonzero reduced coordinate bracket in $\\{z_j,H\\}_D$ is with $\\bar z_j$, so step 2.2 gives $\\dot z_j=\\{z_j,H\\}_D=-i\\hbar^{-1}\\partial H/\\partial\\bar z_j$. Multiplying by $i\\hbar$ proves the stated equation. The calculation remains finite-dimensional for every $N$ and introduces no infinite-dimensional Poisson bracket or fermionic anticommutator.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 2.2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
