    "lem-qft-photon-covariance-spectrum": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-qft-photon-null-quotient",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and use $\\mathcal H_T$ from [[def-qft-photon-transverse-space]]. On $\\mathcal K=L^2(\\mathbb R^3,d^3\\mathbf k;\\mathbb C^4)$ define\n$$[\\epsilon,\\delta]_\\eta=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\epsilon(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger\\eta\\,\\delta(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k,\\qquad\n\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1).$$\nFor $\\mathbf k\\ne0$, let $n=\\mathbf k/|\\mathbf k|$ and set\n$$\\mathcal K_0=\\{\\epsilon\\in\\mathcal K:-\\epsilon^0+n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\epsilon=0\\text{ a.e.}\\},\\qquad\nT\\epsilon=\\boldsymbol\\epsilon-n\\epsilon^0.$$\nThen $\\mathcal K_0$ is closed, $T:\\mathcal K_0\\to\\mathcal H_T$ is bounded and onto, and\n$$[\\epsilon,\\delta]_\\eta=\\langle T\\epsilon,T\\delta\\rangle_2\\quad(\\epsilon,\\delta\\in\\mathcal K_0).$$\nIts radical (the vectors pairing to zero with all of $\\mathcal K_0$) is\n$$\\mathcal N=\\ker T=\\{b(\\mathbf k)(1,n(\\mathbf k)):b\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^3)\\}.$$\nConsequently $\\mathcal K_0/\\mathcal N$ with the induced form is a positive Hilbert space isometric to $\\mathcal H_T$. The form on the four-component auxiliary space is indefinite; only the subsidiary quotient is the positive physical one-particle space.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qft-lorentz-boost-rotation-factorization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Fix an inertial frame of [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]] and identify its vector coordinates with $\\mathbb R^4$ and $\\eta_0=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. For a unit spatial vector $\\mathbf n_B$ and $\\chi\\in\\mathbb R$, define the boost $B_{\\mathbf n_B}(\\chi)$ by $B e_0=\\cosh\\chi\\,e_0+\\sinh\\chi\\,\\mathbf n_B$, $B\\mathbf n_B=\\sinh\\chi\\,e_0+\\cosh\\chi\\,\\mathbf n_B$, and $Bw=w$ for every $w$ Euclidean-orthogonal to $\\operatorname{span}(e_0,\\mathbf n_B)$. Every proper orthochronous linear Lorentz map $\\Lambda\\in SO^+(1,3)$, as defined in [[def-lorentz-map-and-lorentz-group]], admits $\\Lambda=B_{\\mathbf n_B}(\\chi)R_{\\mathbf n_R}(\\theta)$, where $R_{\\mathbf n_R}(\\theta)$ fixes the time axis and $\\mathbf n_R$, and sends $\\mathbf v_\\perp$ to $\\cos\\theta\\,\\mathbf v_\\perp+\\sin\\theta\\,\\mathbf n_R\\times\\mathbf v_\\perp$ on the orthogonal plane, where $\\mathbf v_\\parallel=(\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf n_R)\\mathbf n_R$ and $\\mathbf v_\\perp=\\mathbf v-\\mathbf v_\\parallel$; the cross product is [[def-cross-product-in-r3]]. If $\\Lambda e_0=e_0$, choose $\\chi=0$ and $R_{\\mathbf n_R}(\\theta)=\\Lambda$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let\n$(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a sigma-finite measure space and let\n$\\boldsymbol\\omega=(\\omega_1,\\ldots,\\omega_d):X\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be a\nfinite-valued measurable map, where $d\\ge1$. Put\n$H=L^2(X,\\mu;\\mathbb C)$ with the second-variable-linear pairing of\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]], and form the bosonic Fock space\n$\\mathcal F_s(H)$. For $n\\ge1$, identify $H_s^{(n)}$ with the symmetric\nsubspace of $L^2(X^n,\\overline{\\mu^{\\times n}};\\mathbb C)$ by iterating\n[[lem-qft-l2-product-tensor-identification]]. Set $X^0=\\{\\star\\}$,\n$s_0(\\star)=0$, and for $n\\ge1$ set\n$$s_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)=\\sum_{r=1}^n\\boldsymbol\\omega(x_r)\\in\\mathbb R^d.$$\nFor $1\\le j\\le d$, define $H_j$ by multiplication by the $j$th coordinate\n$s_{n,j}$ on sector $n$, with exact domain\n$$D(H_j)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n): \\sum_{n\\ge1}\\int_{X^n}|s_{n,j}|^2|\\Psi_n|^2\\,d\\overline{\\mu^{\\times n}}<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nEach $H_j$ is self-adjoint, and $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}\\cap D(H_j)$ is a\ngraph core. The joint projection-valued measure\n[[def-projection-valued-measure]] on $\\mathbb R^d$ is\n$$(E(B)\\Psi)_n=\\mathbf1_{s_n^{-1}(B)}\\Psi_n\\quad(n\\ge1),\\qquad (E(B)\\Psi)_0=\\mathbf1_B(0)\\Psi_0.$$\nFor $\\theta\\in\\mathbb R^d$, let $H_\\theta$ be multiplication on sector $n$\nby $\\theta\\cdot s_n$, with its maximal square-integrability domain. Then\n$$U_\\theta(t)\\Psi:=\\bigl(e^{it\\theta\\cdot s_n}\\Psi_n\\bigr)_{n\\ge0}$$\nis a strongly continuous unitary group on all of Fock space, and its norm\nderivative at zero exists exactly for $\\Psi\\in D(H_\\theta)$, where it is\n$iH_\\theta\\Psi$. The zero-particle sector is the vacuum eigenvector at joint\nvalue $0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "cor-compactly-supported-smooth-functions-are-dense-in-wkp-of-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $n\\ge1$, $k\\in\\mathbb N_0$, $1\\le p<\\infty$ and\n$\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$. Then the compactly supported smooth\nfunctions $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb K)$ are dense in\n$W^{k,p}(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb K)$: for every\n$u\\in W^{k,p}(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb K)$ and every $\\delta>0$ there is\n$\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb K)$ with\n$$\\|\\varphi-u\\|_{W^{k,p}(\\mathbb R^n)}<\\delta.$$\nNo such norm-density assertion is made for $p=\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-lorentz-map-and-lorentz-group",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(V,\\eta)$ be a $4$-dimensional Lorentz vector space\n([[def-lorentz-form-and-inertia]]) equipped with a time orientation $C^+$\n([[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]]), and let\n$(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ be the corresponding oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski\nspace ([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]).\n\n**(a) Linear Lorentz maps.** A **linear Lorentz map** is a linear map\n$L:V\\to V$ ([[def-linear-map]]) such that\n\n$$\\eta(Lv,Lw)=\\eta(v,w)\\qquad\\text{for all }v,w\\in V.$$\n\nThe set of all linear Lorentz maps is denoted $O(V,\\eta)$.\n\n**(b) Matrix criterion.** Let $(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ be an ordered\n$\\eta$-orthonormal basis of $V$ ([[lem-lorentz-form-has-orthonormal-basis]]),\nso that $\\eta_0:=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ is the matrix of $\\eta$ in\nthat basis, and let $M$ be the matrix of $L$ in that basis\n([[def-coordinate-column-and-matrix-of-a-linear-map]]). Then $L$ is a linear\nLorentz map if and only if\n\n$$M^{T}\\eta_0 M=\\eta_0,$$\n\nwhere the product is matrix multiplication\n([[def-matrix-product-and-identity-matrix]]). Every linear Lorentz map is a\nlinear isomorphism of $V$ ([[def-linear-isomorphism-and-invertible-linear-map]]),\nand the criterion itself does not depend on the $\\eta$-orthonormal basis used.\nThe three assertions of this clause are proved in\n[[lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure]].\n\n**(c) Proper and orthochronous maps; the Lorentz group.** A linear Lorentz map\n$L$ is **proper** if $\\det L=+1$ ([[def-determinant-of-a-linear-operator]]) and\n**orthochronous** if $L(C^+)=C^+$, a condition on the image of the set $C^+$.\nThe set of proper orthochronous linear Lorentz maps is denoted\n$SO^{+}(V,\\eta)$. The set $O(V,\\eta)$ is called the **(full) Lorentz group** of\n$(V,\\eta)$ and $SO^{+}(V,\\eta)$ the **proper orthochronous Lorentz group**;\nthat these sets are groups under composition is proved in\n[[lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure]], which also shows\nthat $L$ is orthochronous if and only if $L(C^+)$ is contained in $C^+$.\n\n**(d) Affine Lorentz maps.** An **affine Lorentz map** is a map $F:A\\to A$\nfor which there exist $p_0\\in A$ and a linear Lorentz map $L$ with\n\n$$F(p_0+v)=F(p_0)+Lv\\qquad\\text{for all }v\\in V.$$\n\nEquivalently $F(p)=F(p_0)+L(p-p_0)$ for all $p\\in A$, and $L$ is then unique:\nit is recovered as $Lv=F(p+v)-F(p)$ for every $p\\in A$, so it is independent\nof the base event. $F$ is called **proper** (respectively **orthochronous**)\nwhen its linear part $L$ is. If $x$ is the chart of an inertial frame\n([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]), then\n\n$$x(F(p))=M\\,x(p)+b\\qquad\\text{for all }p\\in A,$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the matrix of $L$ in the frame's ordered basis and\n$b:=x(F(o))-Mx(o)\\in\\mathbb R^4$; the two sides are column vectors in\n$\\mathbb R^4$.\n\n**(e) Convention.** In this framework, unless stated otherwise, the term\n**Lorentz transformation** means a proper orthochronous affine Lorentz map.\nImproper or non-orthochronous Lorentz maps are always named explicitly.\n\nThe composition of two affine Lorentz maps is affine Lorentz with linear part\nthe composition of the linear parts, and the identity and the inverse of an\naffine Lorentz map are again such maps; these facts are part of\n[[lem-lorentz-map-preserves-metric-and-causal-structure]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. A natural number is a von Neumann natural, that is a set,\nand $n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ ([[def-natural-numbers]],\n[[lem-nat-order-is-membership]]), so\n\n$$\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\;=\\; \\{\\, x \\;:\\; x \\text{ is a function } n \\to \\mathbb{R} \\,\\}$$\n\nis the function space of [[def-function-space]] at $F = \\mathbb{R}$ and $X = n$,\na vector space over $\\mathbb{R}$ under the pointwise operations\n([[def-vector-space]]). We write $x_k := x(k)$ for $k < n$, and two elements of\n$\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ are equal exactly when they agree at every $k < n$. This is the\nsame set that [[lem-metrics-on-rn]] calls $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$.\n\nThe **Euclidean inner product** of $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is the real number\n\n$$\\langle x, y\\rangle \\;:=\\; \\sum_{k<n} x_k\\,y_k ,$$\n\nthe finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]] applied to the list $k \\mapsto x_k y_k$\n(extended by $0$ beyond $n$, as every finite list in this library is). The\n**Euclidean norm** of $x$ is\n\n$$\\lVert x\\rVert_2 \\;:=\\; \\sqrt{\\langle x, x\\rangle},$$\n\nwhich is defined because $\\langle x,x\\rangle = \\sum_{k<n} x_k^{2} \\ge 0$ (a sum\nof nonnegative terms, [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 4 and\n[[lem-of-square-positive]], the case $x_k = 0$ giving $x_k^2 = 0$ by\n[[def-integer-power]]) and every nonnegative real has a unique nonnegative square\nroot ([[thm-of-square-roots]]).\n\n### Both are defined for every $n$, including $n = 0$\n\nAt $n = 0$ the set $\\mathbb{R}^{0}$ has exactly one element, the empty function,\nand it is the zero vector space ([[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]] clause 5); the sum\nabove is the empty sum, so $\\langle x,y\\rangle = 0$ and $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = 0$.\n**This is the first place on this page where the two index regimes diverge, and\nthe divergence is deliberate.** The published metrics $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ of\n[[lem-metrics-on-rn]] are defined only for $n \\ge 1$, because $d_\\infty$ would\notherwise be a maximum over the empty index set; the algebra above needs no such\nrestriction. The boundary in this page runs between the algebra and the metric,\nnot where a reader would guess, and [[rem-rn-conventions-and-scope]] lists\nexactly which items inherit $n \\ge 1$.\n\n### The algebra of the inner product\n\nFor all $x, y, z \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ and $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}$:\n\n1. **Symmetry.** $\\langle x,y\\rangle = \\langle y,x\\rangle$, since $x_ky_k = y_kx_k$\n   termwise.\n2. **Additivity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle x + y, z\\rangle = \\langle x,z\\rangle + \\langle y,z\\rangle$: the list\n   $k \\mapsto (x_k+y_k)z_k$ is the termwise sum of $k \\mapsto x_kz_k$ and\n   $k \\mapsto y_kz_k$, so [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 1 applies.\n3. **Homogeneity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle \\lambda x, y\\rangle = \\lambda\\langle x,y\\rangle$, by\n   [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 2.\n4. **Bilinearity.** Clauses 2 and 3 together with symmetry give the same two laws\n   in the second argument.\n5. **Positive definiteness.** $\\langle x,x\\rangle \\ge 0$, and\n   $\\langle x,x\\rangle = 0$ if and only if $x = 0$. Indeed a vanishing sum of\n   nonnegative terms has every term $0$ ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 4), so\n   $x_k^{2} = 0$ for every $k < n$, and a nonzero real has a positive square\n   ([[lem-of-square-positive]]), whence $x_k = 0$ for every $k < n$ and $x = 0$.\n6. **Agreement with the published Euclidean metric.** For $n \\ge 1$ and\n   $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$,\n   $\\lVert x - y\\rVert_2 = \\sqrt{\\sum_{k<n}(x_k-y_k)^{2}} = d_2(x,y)$, the two\n   sides being the same expression ([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). In particular\n   $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = d_2(x, 0)$.\n\nThat $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ is a norm in the sense of [[def-norm-and-normed-space]]\nis proved in [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]], where the triangle\ninequality is obtained from the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality; it is not assumed\nhere.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $n \\ge 1$,\nlet $S^{n-1} \\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ be the unit sphere, and let $\\sigma$ be the\nset function of [[def-polar-surface-measure-on-the-unit-sphere]]. Then $\\sigma$\nis a finite Borel measure on $S^{n-1}$, and for every Borel measurable\n$f : \\mathbb R^n \\to [0,\\infty]$,\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^n} f(x)\\,d\\lambda_n(x) = \\int_0^\\infty \\int_{S^{n-1}} f(r\\omega) r^{n-1}\\,d\\sigma(\\omega)\\,dr.$$\nMoreover, $\\sigma$ is the unique Borel measure on $S^{n-1}$ with this\nproperty.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F5], $\\Lambda$ preserves $\\eta$ and sends a future null vector $p$ to a future null vector $\\Lambda p$. It maps $p^\\perp$ onto $(\\Lambda p)^\\perp$ and $\\mathbb Cp$ onto $\\mathbb C\\Lambda p$, so it induces a well-defined map on the quotient fiber. The induced form is preserved because $\\eta(\\Lambda v,\\Lambda w)=\\eta(v,w)$; hence the fiber map is an isometry. Applying two maps successively gives the representation composition law.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F5",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "For the boost $B_n(\\chi)$ in [F2], put $\\beta=\\tanh\\chi$ and $\\gamma=\\cosh\\chi$, so $|\\beta|<1$ and $\\gamma=(1-\\beta^2)^{-1/2}$. In Euclidean unit direction $n$ [F5], its spatial cone coordinates satisfy\n$$\\mathbf k'=\\mathbf k+(\\gamma-1)(n\\cdot\\mathbf k)n-\\gamma\\beta\\omega n,\\qquad\n\\omega'=\\gamma(\\omega-\\beta n\\cdot\\mathbf k).$$\nTaking $n$ as the third coordinate axis, the first two spatial coordinates are unchanged and\n$$\\frac{\\partial k'_3}{\\partial k_3}\n=\\gamma\\left(1-\\beta\\frac{k_3}{\\omega}\\right)=\\frac{\\omega'}{\\omega}.$$\nThus $\\det(\\partial\\mathbf k'/\\partial\\mathbf k)=\\omega'/\\omega$. A rotation has determinant one and preserves $\\omega$. By [F2], every proper orthochronous $\\Lambda$ is a product of these, so $d^3\\mathbf k'/(2\\omega')=d^3\\mathbf k/(2\\omega)$. Combining this change of variables with the fiber isometry in step 1.1 proves unitarity on the quotient-section space and verifies the stated identification with $\\mathcal H_T$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For $v\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C^3)$, consider the bundle section $s_v(p)=\\sqrt{2\\omega}\\,P_T(\\mathbf k)v(\\mathbf k)$; under the stated unitary identification it corresponds exactly to $u=P_Tv\\in\\mathcal H_T$. In the transverse chart the transformed section is $T_p(\\Lambda s_v(\\Lambda^{-1}p))$, where $T_p$ is the explicit continuous fiber quotient map of [F1]. Its support stays in a fixed compact set for Lorentz transformations near the identity. Away from an arbitrarily small ball about $\\mathbf k=0$, the momentum maps and fiber maps vary uniformly continuously; inside that ball $s_v$ is bounded and the $d\\nu$ measure tends to zero with the radius. Hence the transformed sections converge in $L^2(d\\nu)$ as $\\Lambda\\to I$. Such sections are dense: approximate any $u\\in\\mathcal H_T$ by $v\\in C_c^\\infty$ using [F4], and apply the bounded projection $P_T$; the transformed maps are unitary by step 1.2, so density extends continuity to every section. The Fock lift is strongly continuous on finite tensor products and the vacuum by this one-particle continuity; finite-particle vectors are dense, and its unitary bound extends continuity to all Fock space.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 1.2",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "The measure for the one-particle shell is sigma-finite by [F6]. The phase in the statement is multiplication by the four-vector $(\\omega,-\\mathbf k)$. Identify $L^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C^3)$ with scalar $L^2(\\mathbb R^3\\times\\{1,2,3\\})$ using counting measure on the three labels; [F3] then gives the joint PVM with sector multipliers $(\\sum_r|\\mathbf k_r|,-\\sum_r\\mathbf k_r)$ and their exact maximal domains. On each one-particle sector $P_T(\\mathbf k)$ commutes with every scalar multiplier $1_B(|\\mathbf k|,-\\mathbf k)$; hence $P_T^{\\otimes n}$ commutes with the n-particle joint PVM and multiplier. The symmetric transverse sector is reducing, so restriction preserves the exact multiplier domain. Each joint value $(\\sum_r|\\mathbf k_r|,-\\sum_r\\mathbf k_r)$ lies in $\\overline V_+$ by the triangle inequality. Conversely, every nonzero lightlike boundary point is in the support of the one-particle measure, since every neighborhood of its momentum has positive measure and a nonzero transverse fiber. For $E>|\\mathbf P|$, choose $\\mathbf k_1=-\\mathbf P/2+q n$ and $\\mathbf k_2=-\\mathbf P/2-q n$ with $n\\perp\\mathbf P$ and $q=\\tfrac12\\sqrt{E^2-|\\mathbf P|^2}$ (when $\\mathbf P=0$, choose any unit $n$). Then $|\\mathbf k_1|+|\\mathbf k_2|=E$ and $-(\\mathbf k_1+\\mathbf k_2)=\\mathbf P$. Small neighborhoods of these nonzero momenta have positive measure and nonzero transverse fibers, so every neighborhood of $(E,\\mathbf P)$ has nonzero two-particle spectral projection. The vacuum supplies the origin. Thus the joint spectrum is exactly $\\overline V_+$, and [F3] gives the exact energy domain.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 1.2",
            "given"
          ]
        }
      ],
