    "lem-qft-photon-local-field-strength": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-qft-photon-smeared-potential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $j=(j_1,j_2,j_3)\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb R^3)$ and use $\\mathcal H_T$ and $P_T$ from [[def-qft-photon-transverse-space]]. For $\\omega=|\\mathbf k|$ and $\\mathbf k\\ne0$, define\n$$g_j(\\mathbf k)=\\frac{1}{(2\\pi)^{3/2}(2\\omega)^{1/2}}\\,\nP_T(\\mathbf k)\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}j(x)e^{i\\omega x^0-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\,d^4x.$$\nThen $g_j\\in\\mathcal H_T$. On the finite-particle domain $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ of the bosonic Fock space $\\mathcal F_s(\\mathcal H_T)$, the smeared transverse potential is\n$$A_T(j)=a(g_j)+a^\\dagger(g_j).$$\nIts matrix elements are continuous in the test-function topology; it is an operator-valued distribution and does not assign a pointwise operator to $A_i(x)$. With $E_i=-\\partial_0A_i$, the equal-time canonical commutator is the distribution\n$$[A_i(0,\\mathbf x),E_j(0,\\mathbf y)]\n=-i\\,P_{T,ij}(-i\\nabla_{\\mathbf x})\\delta^3(\\mathbf x-\\mathbf y).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qft-photon-covariance-spectrum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. On the future null cone write $p=(\\omega,\\mathbf k)$, $\\omega=|\\mathbf k|$, $\\mathbf k\\ne0$, and use the measure $d\\nu=d^3\\mathbf k/(2\\omega)$. The fiber $p^\\perp/\\mathbb Cp$ has the positive quotient form of [[lem-qft-photon-null-quotient]]. For each proper orthochronous Lorentz transformation $\\Lambda$, the action\n$$[s(p)]\\longmapsto[\\Lambda s(\\Lambda^{-1}p)]$$\nis a unitary representation on the square-integrable quotient sections. It is strongly continuous. Under the unitary identification with $\\mathcal H_T$ given by $u(\\mathbf k)=s(p)/\\sqrt{2\\omega}$, the free translation representation has phase $e^{i(\\omega a^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf a)}$. Its bosonic Fock lift has joint spectrum the closed future cone\n$$\\overline V_+=\\{(E,\\mathbf P):E\\ge|\\mathbf P|\\},$$\nwith the vacuum at $(0,\\mathbf0)$. The energy is the nonnegative sector multiplier $\\sum_{r=1}^n|\\mathbf k_r|$ with the exact maximal square-integrability domain on each sector and square-summability over sectors. These are mathematical free-field representation statements.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. For $\\mu\\ge0$, define the tempered distribution\n$$\\Delta_\\mu(t,\\mathbf x)=\n\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}\n\\frac{\\sin(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)t)}{\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)}\ne^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x},$$\nwith the massless integrand defined off $\\mathbf k=0$; its $1/|\\mathbf k|$\nbehavior is locally integrable in three dimensions. Then\n$$\\operatorname{supp}\\Delta_\\mu\n\\subseteq\\{(t,\\mathbf x):t^2-|\\mathbf x|^2\\ge0\\},$$\nthe closed causal cone for the $(-+++)$ metric. For real compactly supported\nsmooth $f,g$, put\n$$\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)=\n\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\nf(x)g(y)\\Delta_\\mu(x-y)\\,d^4x\\,d^4y.$$\nOn $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ the smeared fields of\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]] satisfy\n$$[\\Phi_\\mu(f),\\Phi_\\mu(g)]=-i\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)I.$$\nConsequently spacelike-separated supports give commuting fields.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Antisymmetry gives\n$$\\frac12\\int(\\partial_aA_b-\\partial_bA_a)h^{ab}\\,d^4x\n=\\int\\partial_aA_b\\,h^{ab}\\,d^4x\n=-\\int A_b\\,\\partial_a h^{ab}\\,d^4x,$$\nwhere integration by parts has no boundary term because $h$ is compactly supported. The current $j^b=-\\partial_a h^{ab}$ obeys $\\partial_bj^b=-\\partial_b\\partial_a h^{ab}=0$, since exchanging $a,b$ changes the sign of $h$. Adding $\\partial_b\\lambda$ to $A_b$ changes the last integral by $\\int\\lambda\\,\\partial_b\\partial_a h^{ab}=0$. The spatial components of $j$ are compact smooth, so [F1] supplies the vector $g_h=g_j$ and the stated finite-particle operator.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "A null-cone polarization representative $\\epsilon_b$ contributes to a field-strength coefficient through $p_a\\epsilon_b-p_b\\epsilon_a$. Replacing $\\epsilon$ by $\\epsilon+c p$ leaves this expression unchanged because $p_ap_b-p_bp_a=0$. Under a Lorentz transformation both $p$ and $\\epsilon$ transform linearly, so their antisymmetric product transforms as a two-form. The unitary quotient action [F2] therefore intertwines the smeared tensor test with its Lorentz pullback, proving covariance without choosing a global polarization frame.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The mode formula in [F1] and the CCR give, after changing $\\mathbf k$ to $-\\mathbf k$ in the negative-frequency term,\n$$[A_i(x),A_j(y)]=\\left(\\delta_{ij}-\\frac{k_i k_j}{|\\mathbf k|^2}\\right)C_0(x-y)$$\nas the spatial Fourier multiplier distribution. Differentiating, using $\\partial_{x^0}\\partial_{y^0}=-\\partial_0^2$, gives $[E_i,E_j]=-\\partial_0^2P_{T,ij}C_0$. The massless equation $\\partial_0^2C_0=\\Delta C_0$ follows by differentiating the sine-over-$|\\mathbf k|$ integral in [F3]. The multiplier of $-\\Delta P_{T,ij}$ is $\\delta_{ij}|\\mathbf k|^2-k_ik_j$, which is exactly the multiplier of $-\\delta_{ij}\\Delta+\\partial_i\\partial_j$; this proves the stated electric commutator. For magnetic fields, each curl kills the longitudinal multiplier $k_sk_u/|\\mathbf k|^2$, and $\\epsilon_{irs}\\epsilon_{jts}k_rk_t=\\delta_{ij}|\\mathbf k|^2-k_ik_j$ gives the same expression. For the mixed commutator, translation invariance gives $\\partial_{y^r}=-\\partial_{x^r}$, so\n$$[E_i(x),B_j(y)]=\\epsilon_{jrs}\\partial_0\\partial_rP_{T,is}C_0.$$\nThe longitudinal term is zero because $\\epsilon_{jrs}k_rk_s=0$, and relabeling the remaining indices gives $\\epsilon_{jri}\\partial_0\\partial_rC_0$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "If a test function is supported in the complement of $\\operatorname{supp}C_0$, each of its derivatives has the same support, and the definition $\\langle\\partial_a C_0,f\\rangle=-\\langle C_0,\\partial_af\\rangle$ shows the derivative distribution also vanishes there. Thus every derivative in step 2.1 remains supported in the closed causal cone from [F3]. For spacelike-separated compact test supports, every point in their difference set lies outside that cone, so pairing each commutator with their tensor product gives zero. The smeared field strengths commute on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "given"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
