    "lem-qft-photon-local-weyl-observables": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-qft-photon-local-field-strength",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $A_0=0$ and let $A_i$ be the transverse potential operator-valued distribution of [[lem-qft-photon-smeared-potential]]. Set\n$$F_{ab}=\\partial_aA_b-\\partial_bA_a,\\qquad E_i=-\\partial_0A_i,\\qquad B_i=\\epsilon_{irs}\\partial_rA_s,$$\nwhere $\\epsilon_{irs}$ is the totally antisymmetric symbol with $\\epsilon_{123}=1$. In the commutator formulas below, $\\Delta=\\sum_{r=1}^3\\partial_r^2$ is the spatial Laplacian acting on $x-y$.\nFor a real compactly supported smooth antisymmetric test tensor $h^{ab}=-h^{ba}$, define\n$$F(h)=\\frac12\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}F_{ab}(x)h^{ab}(x)\\,d^4x\n=-A_b(\\partial_a h^{ab}).$$\nThe effective test is conserved, $\\partial_b\\partial_a h^{ab}=0$, and $F(h)$ is unchanged by $A_b\\mapsto A_b+\\partial_b\\lambda$ for smooth $\\lambda$. Its one-particle coefficient $g_h$ is the vector $g_j$ of [[lem-qft-photon-smeared-potential]] for $j_i=-\\partial_a h^{ai}$, so $F(h)=a(g_h)+a^\\dagger(g_h)$ on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$.\n\nLet $C_0=-i\\Delta_0$, where $\\Delta_0$ is the massless scalar causal commutator of [[lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator]]. As distributions in $x-y$,\n$$[E_i(x),E_j(y)]=[B_i(x),B_j(y)]\n=(-\\delta_{ij}\\Delta+\\partial_i\\partial_j)C_0(x-y),$$\n$$[E_i(x),B_j(y)]=\\epsilon_{jri}\\partial_0\\partial_rC_0(x-y).$$\nEvery right-hand side has support in the closed causal cone. Hence compactly supported tests with spacelike-separated supports give commuting field-strength operators on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. The quotient representation of [[lem-qft-photon-covariance-spectrum]] makes these smeared field strengths Lorentz covariant: their on-shell coefficient depends on a polarization class only through $p_a\\epsilon_b-p_b\\epsilon_a$. These conclusions concern gauge-invariant free observables and do not assert locality of the Coulomb potential.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]] and let $H$ be a separable\ncomplex Hilbert space with the second-variable-linear convention of\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. On $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ define the bosonic\ncreation and annihilation maps $a^\\dagger(h)$ and $a(h)$ and the fermionic maps\n$c^\\dagger(h)$ and $c(h)$ as in\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. Write $a_n(h)$ and $a_n^\\dagger(h)$ for\ntheir restrictions to the $n$th bosonic sector. For bosons set\n\n$$D(a(h))=\\{\\Psi\\in\\mathcal F_s(H):\\sum_{n\\ge1}\\|a_n(h)\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\},\\qquad D(a^\\dagger(h))=\\{\\Psi\\in\\mathcal F_s(H):\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\|a_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\}.$$\n\nThen $\\mathcal F_s(H)$ and $\\mathcal F_a(H)$ are Hilbert spaces,\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is dense, and it is a graph core for both maximal\nbosonic sector maps. The bosonic operators on their displayed domains are\nclosed and mutually adjoint, and on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ satisfy\n\n$$[a(h),a^\\dagger(k)]=\\langle h,k\\rangle_2I,\\qquad [a(h),a(k)]=[a^\\dagger(h),a^\\dagger(k)]=0.$$\n\nThe fermionic $c(h)$ and $c^\\dagger(h)$ extend to bounded operators on the\nwhole Fock space, with norms $\\|h\\|$, and satisfy on the whole space\n\n$$\\{c(h),c^\\dagger(k)\\}=\\langle h,k\\rangle_2I,\\qquad \\{c(h),c(k)\\}=\\{c^\\dagger(h),c^\\dagger(k)\\}=0.$$\n\nThis is a mathematical construction of the two Fock representations; it does\nnot assign particle statistics to a physical species.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-rational-box-step-functions-form-a-countable-dense-subset-of-l-p-of-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice.\n\nLet $1 \\le p < \\infty$. The finite linear combinations of indicator functions of\nhalf-open boxes with rational endpoints and rational coefficients form a\ncountable dense subset of $L^p(\\mathbb{R}^n)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let $H$ be a separable\ncomplex Hilbert space with the second-variable-linear convention of\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]], and let $\\mathcal F_s(H)$ and\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ be its bosonic Fock space and finite-particle domain.\nFor $h\\in H$ define on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ the Segal field\n$$\\phi_0(h):=2^{-1/2}\\bigl(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h)\\bigr).$$\nFor $h\\ne0$, this symmetric operator is essentially self-adjoint; write\n$\\Phi(h)=\\overline{\\phi_0(h)}$. If $r=\\|h\\|$, $e=h/r$, $K=e^\\perp$,\n$G=\\mathcal F_s(K)$, choose a countable orthonormal basis $(u_j)$ of $G$\nwhose vectors are finite-particle, and use the one-mode splitting of\n[[lem-qft-fock-one-mode-splitting]] followed by the Hermite unitary\n$|n\\rangle\\mapsto g_n$ into $L^2(\\mathbb R,dx;\\mathbb C)$ with pairing\n$\\langle f,g\\rangle_2=\\int\\overline f g$ to write a vector as\n$(f_j)_{j\\in J}\\in\\ell^2(J;L^2(\\mathbb R,dx;\\mathbb C))$. Then the exact domain and\naction are\n$$D(\\Phi(h))=\\left\\{(f_j):\\sum_{j\\in J}\\int_{\\mathbb R}(1+r^2x^2)|f_j(x)|^2\\,dx<\\infty\\right\\},\\qquad (\\Phi(h)f)_j(x)=rx f_j(x).$$\nFor $h=0$, $\\phi_0(0)$ closes to the zero operator on all of $\\mathcal F_s(H)$.\n\nDefine $\\epsilon(z)=\\bigoplus_{n\\ge0}z^{\\otimes n}/\\sqrt{n!}$ for $z\\in H$.\nThere are unitaries $W(h)$ determined on these total exponential vectors by\n$$W(h)\\epsilon(z)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\|h\\|^2}{4} +\\frac{i}{\\sqrt2}\\langle h,z\\rangle_2\\right) \\epsilon\\!\\left(z+\\frac{i h}{\\sqrt2}\\right).$$\nThey obey\n$$W(h)W(k)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{i}{2}\\operatorname{Im}\\langle h,k\\rangle_2\\right)W(h+k),\\qquad \\langle\\Omega,W(h)\\Omega\\rangle_2=e^{-\\|h\\|^2/4},$$\nand $h\\mapsto W(h)$ is strongly continuous. For every $h$, the one-parameter\ngroup $t\\mapsto W(th)$ has norm derivative at zero exactly on\n$D(\\Phi(h))$, with derivative $i\\Phi(h)$ (for $h=0$ this is the zero\ngenerator). Thus the displayed bounded Weyl operators have precisely the\nclosed Segal fields as their generators; no point-field or measurement claim\nis included.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Let $D$ be the real countable dense family in [F3]. Then $D+iD$ is countable and dense in complex scalar $L^2$, because the squared norm of a complex error is the sum of the squared real- and imaginary-part errors. Its finite triple product is countable and dense in $L^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C^3)$. If $(v_n)$ enumerates that dense set, boundedness of $P_T$ makes $(P_Tv_n)$ dense in $\\operatorname{Ran}P_T=\\mathcal H_T$: for $u=P_Tu$, any $v_n\\to u$ implies $P_Tv_n\\to u$. Thus the separability hypothesis of [F4] holds, and the Segal supplier gives a bounded unitary $W_{\\rm Segal}(u)$ for every $u\\in\\mathcal H_T$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "From [F1]–[F2], on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$,\n$$[F(h),F(k)]=[a(g_h)+a^\\dagger(g_h),a(g_k)+a^\\dagger(g_k)]\n=(\\langle g_h,g_k\\rangle_2-\\langle g_k,g_h\\rangle_2)I\n=2i\\,\\operatorname{Im}\\langle g_h,g_k\\rangle_2\\,I.$$\nFor spacelike-separated supports the left side vanishes by [F1], hence $\\operatorname{Im}\\langle g_h,g_k\\rangle_2=0$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The supplier's one-parameter group generated by the closed Segal field gives $W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2g_h)$ with generator $\\sqrt2\\,\\Phi(g_h)=\\overline{a(g_h)+a^\\dagger(g_h)}$, which is the closure of $F(h)$ from [F1]. Thus $W_O(h)$ is the bounded Weyl observable associated to this field strength. It is unitary, and its real and imaginary parts are bounded self-adjoint operators with norm at most one.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "The Weyl relation [F4] gives\n$$W_O(h)W_O(k)=e^{-i\\,\\operatorname{Im}\\langle\\sqrt2g_h,\\sqrt2g_k\\rangle_2/2}W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2g_h+\\sqrt2g_k).$$\nBy step 1.2 the phase is one for spacelike-separated tests, so $W_O(h)$ and $W_O(k)$ commute. Products, adjoints, and norm limits preserve commutation; therefore the norm-closed algebras of two spacelike-separated regions commute. Since $W_{\\rm Segal}(0)=I$, these algebras are unital.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "step 1.2",
            "given"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
