    "ex-qft-qed-two-polarizations": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-photon-transverse-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let\n$$\\mathcal H=L^2(\\mathbb R^3,d^3\\mathbf k;\\mathbb C^3),\\qquad\n\\langle u,v\\rangle_2=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\overline{u(\\mathbf k)}\\cdot v(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k,$$\nwith the second-variable-linear convention of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]; the dot product and norm on each $\\mathbb R^3$ fiber are those of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]], and Hilbert completeness follows from [[lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space]]. For $\\mathbf k\\ne0$, write $n(\\mathbf k)=\\mathbf k/|\\mathbf k|$ and\n$$P_T(\\mathbf k)=I_3-n(\\mathbf k)n(\\mathbf k)^T.$$\nAssign any value at $\\mathbf k=0$, which is a Lebesgue-null set. Let $M_{P_T}$ be multiplication by this measurable matrix field and define the positive transverse photon space\n$$\\mathcal H_T=\\operatorname{Ran}M_{P_T}\n=\\{u\\in\\mathcal H:\\mathbf k\\cdot u(\\mathbf k)=0\\text{ for almost every }\\mathbf k\\ne0\\}.$$\nThe inner product on $\\mathcal H_T$ is the restriction of that on $\\mathcal H$. Each nonzero momentum fiber is the two-dimensional plane perpendicular to $\\mathbf k$; no global continuous polarization frame is part of the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qft-photon-null-quotient",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and use $\\mathcal H_T$ from [[def-qft-photon-transverse-space]]. On $\\mathcal K=L^2(\\mathbb R^3,d^3\\mathbf k;\\mathbb C^4)$ define\n$$[\\epsilon,\\delta]_\\eta=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\epsilon(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger\\eta\\,\\delta(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k,\\qquad\n\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1).$$\nFor $\\mathbf k\\ne0$, let $n=\\mathbf k/|\\mathbf k|$ and set\n$$\\mathcal K_0=\\{\\epsilon\\in\\mathcal K:-\\epsilon^0+n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\epsilon=0\\text{ a.e.}\\},\\qquad\nT\\epsilon=\\boldsymbol\\epsilon-n\\epsilon^0.$$\nThen $\\mathcal K_0$ is closed, $T:\\mathcal K_0\\to\\mathcal H_T$ is bounded and onto, and\n$$[\\epsilon,\\delta]_\\eta=\\langle T\\epsilon,T\\delta\\rangle_2\\quad(\\epsilon,\\delta\\in\\mathcal K_0).$$\nIts radical (the vectors pairing to zero with all of $\\mathcal K_0$) is\n$$\\mathcal N=\\ker T=\\{b(\\mathbf k)(1,n(\\mathbf k)):b\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^3)\\}.$$\nConsequently $\\mathcal K_0/\\mathcal N$ with the induced form is a positive Hilbert space isometric to $\\mathcal H_T$. The form on the four-component auxiliary space is indefinite; only the subsidiary quotient is the positive physical one-particle space.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qft-photon-no-global-polarization-frame",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. For $n\\in S^2\\subset\\mathbb R^3$, let $E_n=n^\\perp=\\operatorname{Ran}(I-nn^T)$ be the real transverse plane. There do not exist continuous maps $e_1,e_2:S^2\\to\\mathbb R^3$ such that, for every $n$, $(e_1(n),e_2(n))$ is an orthonormal basis of $E_n$. A continuous real orthonormal polarization frame would in particular give a continuous nowhere-zero tangent vector field on $S^2$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-cross-product-in-r3",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $u=(u_x,u_y,u_z)$ and $v=(v_x,v_y,v_z)$ in $\\mathbb R^3$, define $u\\times v=(u_yv_z-u_zv_y,\\,u_zv_x-u_xv_z,\\,u_xv_y-u_yv_x)$.\n\nThis is the right-handed **cross product**. The displayed coordinates are those of the standard basis ([[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]]), and inner products and norms are those of [[def-euclidean-inner-product]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-cross-product-is-bilinear-alternating-and-orthogonal",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all $u,v,w\\in\\mathbb R^3$, the cross product is bilinear and alternating, $\\langle u\\times v,w\\rangle=\\det[u\\ v\\ w]$, and $u\\times v$ is orthogonal to both $u$ and $v$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-cross-product-norm-is-the-two-vector-gram-determinant",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $u,v\\in\\mathbb R^3$, $\\|u\\times v\\|_2^2=\\det G(u,v)$, and this value is positive exactly when $u$ and $v$ are linearly independent.\n\nEquivalently,\n$$\\|u\\times v\\|_2^2=\\|u\\|_2^2\\|v\\|_2^2-\\langle u,v\\rangle^2.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Since $n$ is unit, $P_T(n)=I-nn^T$ has kernel $\\mathbb R n$ and range $n^\\perp$, of dimension $3-1=2$, by [F1]. For any fixed unit vector $u$ not parallel to $n_0$, $P_T(n)u$ is nonzero on a neighborhood of $n_0$; there define $e_1(n)=P_T(n)u/|P_T(n)u|$ and $e_2(n)=n\\times e_1(n)$. By [F4], $e_2$ is orthogonal to both unit vectors and $|e_2|^2=1-0=1$. It is therefore a continuous unit vector in $n^\\perp$, orthogonal to $e_1$, so the two vectors form a local frame.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "The quotient in [F2] is positive and isometric to the range in step 1.1, so it has exactly two physical directions in each nonzero momentum fiber. A global continuous real frame would contradict [F3]. Thus the local construction cannot be made into a single continuous frame over the whole sphere.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement does not assert a case about an empty set or empty region; its objects and quantifiers are stated explicitly."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The polarization vectors in the local frame are required to have unit norm."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 1.1 constructs exactly two local unit orthogonal vectors e_1 and e_2."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No additional degenerate parameter or rank case is included beyond the stated nondegeneracy hypotheses."
        },
        {
