    "cex-qft-qed-coulomb-potential-locality": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-qft-photon-smeared-potential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $j=(j_1,j_2,j_3)\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb R^3)$ and use $\\mathcal H_T$ and $P_T$ from [[def-qft-photon-transverse-space]]. For $\\omega=|\\mathbf k|$ and $\\mathbf k\\ne0$, define\n$$g_j(\\mathbf k)=\\frac{1}{(2\\pi)^{3/2}(2\\omega)^{1/2}}\\,\nP_T(\\mathbf k)\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}j(x)e^{i\\omega x^0-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\,d^4x.$$\nThen $g_j\\in\\mathcal H_T$. On the finite-particle domain $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ of the bosonic Fock space $\\mathcal F_s(\\mathcal H_T)$, the smeared transverse potential is\n$$A_T(j)=a(g_j)+a^\\dagger(g_j).$$\nIts matrix elements are continuous in the test-function topology; it is an operator-valued distribution and does not assign a pointwise operator to $A_i(x)$. With $E_i=-\\partial_0A_i$, the equal-time canonical commutator is the distribution\n$$[A_i(0,\\mathbf x),E_j(0,\\mathbf y)]\n=-i\\,P_{T,ij}(-i\\nabla_{\\mathbf x})\\delta^3(\\mathbf x-\\mathbf y).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qft-photon-transverse-projector-kernel",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $G(\\mathbf x)=1/(4\\pi|\\mathbf x|)$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$, interpreted as its locally integrable distribution, and use the Fourier convention\n$$\\widehat f(\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}f(\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x,\\qquad\nf(\\mathbf x)=(2\\pi)^{-3}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\widehat f(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k.$$\nThen $\\mathcal F^{-1}(|\\mathbf k|^{-2})=G$ distributionally. Consequently, for $P_{T,ij}(\\mathbf k)=\\delta_{ij}-k_i k_j/|\\mathbf k|^2$,\n$$P_{T,ij}(-i\\nabla)\\delta^3\n=\\delta_{ij}\\delta^3+\\partial_i\\partial_jG.$$\nFor $\\mathbf x\\ne0$,\n$$\\partial_i\\partial_jG(\\mathbf x)\n=-\\frac{\\delta_{ij}}{4\\pi|\\mathbf x|^3}\n+\\frac{3x_ix_j}{4\\pi|\\mathbf x|^5}.$$\nThus the equal-time transverse-potential commutator of [[lem-qft-photon-smeared-potential]] has this off-diagonal Newtonian kernel (with its stated factor $-i$). The identity is distributional at the origin; the displayed classical derivative formula is only asserted away from it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-smooth-bump-between-concentric-euclidean-balls",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $0<r<R$ and $n\\ge 1$. Then there exists a smooth function $\\rho:\\mathbb R^n\\to [0,1]$ such that $\\rho=1$ on $\\overline B_r(0)$ and $\\operatorname{supp}(\\rho)\\subseteq B_R(0)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Choose a nonzero point $z_0$ on the $x_1$ axis. By [F2], the $11$ commutator kernel is continuous and strictly positive on a sufficiently small neighborhood of $z_0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Choose nonnegative smooth tests $f,g$ from [F3], supported in small disjoint balls centered at points whose difference is $z_0$, with the difference of their supports contained in that neighborhood. The delta term in [F1] pairs to zero because the supports are disjoint, while the integral against the strictly positive off-diagonal kernel from step 1.1 is positive. Hence the commutator pairing is nonzero for disjoint supports, so its kernel is not supported on the diagonal. The potential is nonlocal; the separate field-strength locality result is unaffected.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "given"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
