    "cex-qft-qed-torus-charge": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-qft-qed-coulomb-constraint",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and the conventions of [[def-qft-qed-gauge-data]]. On the periodic box $T_L^3=(\\mathbb R/L\\mathbb Z)^3$, take smooth real periodic charge and current densities $\\rho,\\mathbf J$, smooth periodic $\\mathbf A,\\dot{\\mathbf A},\\phi$, and $L,\\epsilon_0,\\mu_0>0$. Define the formal classical density\n$$\\mathcal L=\\frac{\\epsilon_0}{2}|\\dot{\\mathbf A}+\\nabla\\phi|^2-\\frac1{2\\mu_0}|\\nabla\\times\\mathbf A|^2-\\rho\\phi+\\mathbf J\\cdot\\mathbf A.$$\nThe canonical momenta are $\\boldsymbol\\pi_A=\\epsilon_0(\\dot{\\mathbf A}+\\nabla\\phi)$ and $\\pi_\\phi=0$. Periodic integration by parts gives\n$$H=\\int_{T_L^3}\\left(\\frac{|\\boldsymbol\\pi_A|^2}{2\\epsilon_0}+\\frac{|\\nabla\\times\\mathbf A|^2}{2\\mu_0}\n+\\phi(\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\pi_A+\\rho)-\\mathbf J\\cdot\\mathbf A\\right)d^3x.$$\nVariation of the multiplier $\\phi$ imposes $\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\pi_A+\\rho=0$. In Coulomb gauge $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf A=0$, this becomes $-\\Delta\\phi=\\rho/\\epsilon_0$. Such a smooth periodic solution exists only if $\\int_{T_L^3}\\rho\\,d^3x=0$. If this neutrality condition holds, there is a unique zero-mean solution, with Fourier coefficients\n$$\\widehat\\phi_0=0,\\qquad\n\\widehat\\phi_k=\\frac{\\widehat\\rho_k}{\\epsilon_0|2\\pi k/L|^2}\\quad(k\\ne0),\\qquad\n\\widehat\\rho_k=L^{-3}\\int_{[0,L]^3}\\rho(x)e^{-2\\pi i k\\cdot x/L}\\,d^3x.$$\nIt is smooth and satisfies\n$$\\frac{\\epsilon_0}{2}\\int|\\nabla\\phi|^2\\,d^3x\n=\\frac12\\int\\rho\\phi\\,d^3x\n=\\frac{L^3}{2\\epsilon_0}\\sum_{k\\ne0}\\frac{|\\widehat\\rho_k|^2}{|2\\pi k/L|^2}\\ge0.$$\nThis is a classical conditional solver for smooth periodic neutral data; it makes no claim about quantum density products or nonperiodic boundary conditions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Integrating [F1] over $[0,L]^3$ gives\n$$\\int_{[0,L]^3}-\\Delta\\phi\\,d^3x\n=-\\sum_{j=1}^3\\int_0^{L}\\!\\int_{[0,L]^2}\\partial_j^2\\phi\\,d^2x_{\\widehat j}\\,dx_j=0,$$\nbecause for each $j$ the integral in $x_j$ is the difference of the periodic values of $\\partial_j\\phi$ at $L$ and $0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "The right side of the same integrated equation is\n$$\\epsilon_0^{-1}\\int_{[0,L]^3}\\rho_0\\,d^3x=\\epsilon_0^{-1}\\rho_0L^3\\ne0,$$\ncontradicting step 1.1. Thus no smooth periodic solution exists for this nonzero total charge; the same integration proves neutrality is necessary for any periodic solution.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "given"
          ]
        }
      ],
